/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   ZeroStick EXO — THE KIT.  zs-kit.css

   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   HOW TO LOAD IT.  This is not a preference, it is the difference between the
   file working and appearing to do nothing.

   phone.html's real stylesheet order, read out of the file:

       line    7   <style>            the palette + the component kit
       line  606   zs-brand.css       materials, press, the ion accent
       line  607   <style>            reduced-motion entrances
       line 2434   zs-anim.css        ── IN THE BODY
       line 2440   zs-micro.css       ── IN THE BODY
       line 2441   zs-kit.css   ← THIS FILE, immediately after zs-micro.css

   TWO STYLESHEETS LOAD FROM THE BODY. A <link> in <head> is therefore NOT
   last, and every equal-specificity rule here would silently lose to
   zs-micro.css. The tag goes on line 2441:

       <link rel="stylesheet" href="zs-micro.css">
       <script src="zs-micro.js"></script>
       <link rel="stylesheet" href="zs-kit.css">

   The body slot buys one more thing: it also beats the two stylesheets that
   JavaScript injects at RUNTIME — smart-actions.js `zs-smart-css` and
   phone-sections.js `cv-region-css`, both appended to document.head. Those
   land after everything in <head>, so a head link would have to fight them
   with prefixes; from the body we simply win on order.

   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WHAT WAS WRONG, MEASURED.

   Nine "surfaces" were the same fill separated by 2px of corner radius. A
   card edge measured 1.12:1 against its own fill — not subtle, absent. The
   section header was 12px/700 over a 14.5px/400 row title, so the label was
   louder than the thing it named. Four "pick one of N" controls painted their
   selected state as the same flat cyan slab as the primary button, so on one
   screen a cyan block meant "which tab", "which mode", "which filter" AND
   "press this". And the most-emitted component in the product — .note, 207
   call sites across the two renderers — was a grey paragraph in a
   PlayStation-blue box, on Xbox screens too.

   Everything looked like one thing because, measured, everything WAS one
   thing.

   FOUR MOVES, in order of how much they matter:

   1. TYPE DOES THE WORK. Seven sizes, three weights (400/600/700 — the only
      three every system stack actually has; Segoe UI silently rounds 500 and
      650, so a six-weight scale renders as three unpredictable ones). A
      config name is 26px/700 over an 11.5px/700 tracked eyebrow. That is a
      2.26x ratio, and the hierarchy stops being upside down.

   2. FOUR MATERIALS, EACH WITH A JOB.
        GROUND   the page.                --bg, untouched
        CARD     groups things.           card overlay, --line edge, lit top
        CONTROL  you press it.            control overlay, --zs-edge, a wall
        WELL     holds a selection.       negative overlay, a groove
      They differ in fill AND edge AND elevation, so they survive greyscale.

   3. CYAN MEANS ONE THING AGAIN. A solid ion fill is "press this to do the
      thing" and nothing else. Selection became a raised neutral pill with an
      ion rail under it — a different SHAPE as well as a different colour.

   4. THE MARK DOES WORK. The reticle's corner bracket introduces every
      section; the reticle itself is engraved into the hero. Before this the
      brand appeared on exactly one element in the whole product, which is why
      the app read as anonymous.

   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   CONTRAST. Every pair below was computed, not estimated — the numbers in the
   comments come from a script that composites the actual overlays and runs
   the WCAG formula. Nothing here puts text under 4.5:1 or a control boundary
   under 3:1, in any of the six modes this app can be in. Where a number is
   deliberately below 3:1 it says so and says why.

   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WHAT THIS FILE WILL NOT TOUCH, because something else owns it and stomping
   it would break shipped behaviour. Full pseudo-element census taken across
   phone.html, zs-brand.css, zs-anim.css and zs-micro.css:

     .appbar::after / .phead::after   zs-brand — the chrome fade
     .sheet .shead::before            zs-brand — the drag grabber
     .sw::after                       phone.html — the knob (travel is
                                      hardcoded translateX 22px / 28px)
     .sw::before                      zs-micro:47 — the ion bloom  (see §9.1:
                                      this file adjusts it rather than
                                      replacing it, and that is the only
                                      reason the 44px fix is legitimate)
     .btn.primary::after              zs-micro:116 — the commit glow
     .pk::after                       zs-micro:155 — the lock ring
     .stp .sv::after                  phone.html — content:attr(data-suffix)
     .ib::after                       phone.html — the hit-area expander
     .aimpage .fld::after             phone.html — a clearfix
     .item .ico.ico-*                 phone.html:589 — the five editor hues,
                                      at (0,3,0). Already shipping. §5.3.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */


/* ══ 1. TOKENS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

:root {

  /* ── 1.1 TYPE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Seven sizes, three weights, tracking specified per size because letters
     drift apart as they grow. This continues the rule zs-brand.css set; it
     just now has sizes attached to it. */
  --fs-hero:    26px;   --fw-hero:    700; --lh-hero:    1.10; --tr-hero:   -.028em;
  --fs-title:   19px;   --fw-title:   700; --lh-title:   1.20; --tr-t:      -.020em;
  --fs-lead:  17.5px;   --fw-lead:    700; --lh-lead:    1.22; --tr-l:      -.014em;
  --fs-row:   15.5px;   --fw-row:     600; --lh-row:     1.30; --tr-r:      -.006em;
  --fs-body:  14.5px;   --fw-body:    400; --lh-body:    1.45; --tr-b:       0;
  --fs-sub:     13px;   --fw-sub:     400; --lh-sub:     1.55; --tr-s:      .004em;
  --fs-eyebrow: 11.5px; --fw-eyebrow: 700; --lh-eyebrow: 1.20; --tr-e:      .13em;
  --fs-micro:  10.5px;

  /* The instrument face. Aim speed, deadzone, Hz, ms, uptime — all of it
     changes in place many times a second, and a proportional face makes a
     live readout jitter horizontally as digits swap. Every one of these ships
     with the OS, so it costs zero bytes on a phone that may be on Bluetooth.

     APPLIED NARROWLY, and the narrowness is the point: .flab .rv and
     .stp .sv only. I checked the other candidates against the renderers and
     they carry WORDS — .stat b holds "asking your board…", "● On",
     "checking…", "3 of 4"; .smval holds preset names like "Standard". Mono on
     a sentence is a costume, not a gauge. */
  --zs-mono: ui-monospace, "SF Mono", SFMono-Regular, "Cascadia Mono",
             "Segoe UI Mono", "Roboto Mono", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;

  /* ── 1.2 SPACING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Four values. The kit had sixty distinct padding declarations, which is
     why phone.html needs ten hand-written adjacency rules at the bottom of
     its <style> to patch the rhythm they destroyed.

     --sp-1h IS NOT A FIFTH RHYTHM VALUE, it is the TOUCH FLOOR. Apple's HIG
     and this project's own UX data both ask for >= 8px of clear space
     between two adjacent touch targets, and --sp-1 (4px) is half of that.
     Every place two tappable things sit side by side takes --sp-1h, and
     nothing else does — so a future edit that reaches for it is announcing
     "these two are both targets" rather than "I wanted a slightly bigger
     gap". §14.4 grows it again for body.a11y-big. */
  --sp-1:  4px;   /* inside a chip; label → sub                   */
  --sp-1h: 8px;   /* THE MINIMUM between two adjacent TARGETS     */
  --sp-2: 10px;   /* between siblings in a row; control padding   */
  --sp-3: 16px;   /* card padding; the standard gutter            */
  --sp-4: 26px;   /* between one section and the next             */

  /* ── 1.3 RADIUS AND THE SHEAR ────────────────────────────────────────────
     The three existing tokens are re-pointed rather than replaced, so all ~21
     call sites move at once and the 30 hardcoded radii scattered through the
     kit get restated below.

     THE SHEAR is the one piece of pure identity in the geometry: the mark is
     a heavy sheared italic, so a control cuts its top-right and bottom-left
     corners almost square and rounds the other two. It is one border-radius
     shorthand, it survives borders and box-shadows where clip-path would not,
     and it is the cheapest way to make a rectangle look machined.

     IT IS DEPLOYED ON CONTROLS ONLY, NOT CARDS, and that restraint is
     deliberate. The Overview screen draws eleven cards; eleven sheared
     silhouettes plus their edges is a costume, not a product. Buttons are
     few, and "the boxes the buttons sit in" is the thing the owner named. So
     the language is: containers are soft, controls are machined. */
  --r-chip:   8px;
  --r-ctl:   12px;
  --r-card:  18px;
  --r-sheet: 26px;
  --r-pill:  999px;
  --zs-cut:  2px;
  --zs-shear-ctl:  var(--r-ctl) var(--zs-cut) var(--r-ctl) var(--zs-cut);
  /* .hero and .cfg carry the inline console-family stripe on their left edge.
     Squaring the two LEFT corners makes that stripe read as a solid machined
     bar instead of a curved sliver — it strengthens the signal from rule 7
     while touching nothing but border-radius. */
  --zs-shear-rail: var(--zs-cut) var(--zs-cut) var(--r-card) var(--zs-cut);

  /* ── 1.4 EDGES — two tiers, and the honest reason ────────────────────────
     WCAG 1.4.11 asks 3:1 of a boundary that IDENTIFIES a control or a state.
     It does not ask it of a decorative grouping edge, and a 3:1 grey outline
     around all eleven cards on Overview reads as a wireframe.

     2026-08-18 — ALL THREE WERE RAISED ONE STEP, because §1.5 raised the
     fills underneath them. A brighter surface eats an edge: every number
     below was recomputed against the new composites, not carried over.

     --line     CONTAINERS   #5c7089   (was #485b73)
                  3.88:1 vs --bg · 2.96:1 vs a card top · 3.63:1 vs the page
                  well · 1.84:1 vs a selected pill.
                Baseline was #212b39 at 1.12:1 against the card it bordered,
                i.e. photometrically not there. Deliberately still short of
                3:1 on a card: this is a decorative GROUPING edge, and a card
                is never identified by its outline alone — it has fill, sheen
                and elevation. The old note said "if the cards still read
                flat, THIS is the first value to raise, before anything else
                in the file." The owner said they still read flat. Raised.

     --zs-edge  CONTROLS     #7387a3   (was #657791)
                  vs the LIGHT END of its own control gradient (#32373f) 3.26
                  vs the dark end (#1f242d)                             4.60
                  vs --bg                                               5.37
                  vs a card top (#21272f)                               4.10
                  vs the page well (#111419)                            5.03

     THE INWARD NUMBER IS THE ONE EVERYBODY MISSED, and it is the number that
     set this value. 1.4.11 wants 3:1 against adjacent colours, and the top of
     a button's own fill is adjacent. #657791 on the NEW control face measures
     2.62:1 — so raising the fill without raising the edge would have shipped
     a control boundary that fails, which is exactly the trade every "just add
     more contrast" pass makes without noticing. 3.26:1 solves it inward, and
     inward is the tight side; every outward adjacency clears 4:1.

     --zs-edge-hi  STATE EDGES  #8296b0   (was #6d8099)
                One step brighter again, and it exists for exactly one job:
                the boundary of a SELECTED segment. That pill sits on a
                recessed track, so its edge has three adjacencies rather
                than two, and --zs-edge measures 2.54:1 against the new
                pill's own fill — under 1.4.11 on the one boundary in the
                app that carries "which one is on".
                  vs the selected pill #43474e  3.08
                  vs a control face    #32373f  3.96
                  vs a card top                 4.97
                  vs the page ground            6.51

                ONE CAVEAT, ON THE RECORD RATHER THAN GLOSSED. §8 stacks an
                ion (or, under §17, a section) WASH on top of that pill, and
                against the washed fill (#405b60) this edge reads 2.41:1 —
                under 1.4.11. It was 2.35:1 before this change, so the
                ladder improves it rather than causing it. It is not fixed
                from here because the token has exactly one consumer and
                pushing it to ~#a3b3c9 would put a 8.2:1 outline round the
                selected tab — louder than the state it marks. The pill's
                3px ION RAIL is the non-text indicator that carries the
                requirement: 4.14:1 against the washed fill, plus --text at
                6.33:1, plus weight, plus elevation. The border is the
                fourth of five redundant channels, not the load-bearing
                one. If the wash is ever tuned, re-measure the RAIL first. */
  --line:        #5c7089;
  --zs-edge:     #7387a3;
  --zs-edge-hi:  #8296b0;

  /* ── 1.5 MATERIALS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Overlays, never hex fills. An overlay composites onto whatever --panel
     currently is, so body.zs-oled keeps its true black and still gets the
     same RELATIVE lift; a hardcoded #1c222a would freeze OLED mode into
     looking like the default theme, which is how a theme quietly dies.

     ── 2026-08-18: THE LADDER WAS RE-CUT. The owner: "the background and the
     buttons are all the same color". He is right, and here is the number the
     old values produced — a button face sitting on a card:

         CARD top #1c222a → CONTROL top #21272f       1.06:1

     1.06:1 is a 6% luminance difference. On a 375px phone in daylight that is
     not "subtle", it is ABSENT. The kit compensated with edges, a 2px wall
     and gradients, and those are real — but they are all OUTLINE channels,
     and the FILL channel was doing nothing. Fill is what the eye reads first
     at arm's length.

     Composited on the default --panel #0f151e (measured, not estimated):
                        was          now
       card top      #1c222a  →  #21272f
       card bottom   #141a23  →  #161c25
       ctl  top      #21272f  →  #32373f
       ctl  bottom   #161c25  →  #1f242d
       selected pill #2e333b  →  #43474e
       well on a card         →  #0a0e14
       well on the page       →  #111419   (on --bg, where it is used)

     THE HEADLINE: card → button goes 1.06:1 → 1.26:1, and page → button
     1.30:1 → 1.64:1. "Background and buttons are the same colour" stops being
     true at the FILL level, not only at the outline level.

     THE HONEST CAVEAT, STATED ONCE. On a near-black ground you CANNOT stack
     three raised tiers each ~1.3:1 apart and still carry AA secondary text:
     to put CONTROL 1.30:1 above a CARD that is itself 1.30:1 above the
     GROUND, the control face must reach L≈.040 (#3f444b), where --muted
     measures 3.22:1 and FAILS. So the rule that replaces "add more greys" is
     structural, and it is the load-bearing sentence of this section:

         A CONTROL NEVER SITS ON A CARD THAT SITS ON A CARD. Two raised
         tiers, ever. Separation past that comes from edge + wall + hue,
         never from a third fill.

     That is also why the PAGE well drops from .05 to .035: it now has to read
     as BELOW the ground (1.07:1 under it), not as a fourth grey competing
     with the card.

     THE CONTROL TOP IS .145, and that single number is set by --muted. At
     .145 the new --muted (#93a1b4, raised alongside — see phone.html's
     :root) measures 4.56:1 on a control face, PASSING AA. The old --muted
     #8695a8 measures 3.92:1 there and fails, which is why it moved in the
     same edit rather than in a later one. The principle the old comment got
     right is kept: AA on a button face is preserved BY CONSTRUCTION, not by
     a standing rule someone has to remember. A rule you have to remember is
     a bug with a delay on it.

     ONE PROHIBITION FALLS OUT OF THE TABLE: --muted may NOT sit on a
     SELECTED pill (3.55:1). It does not today — §8 gives the selected
     segment color:var(--text) at 8.11:1 — so this is a guard to keep, not a
     fix to make. */
  --zs2-card-a: rgba(255,255,255,.075);
  --zs2-card-b: rgba(255,255,255,.030);
  --zs2-ctl-a:  rgba(255,255,255,.145);
  --zs2-ctl-b:  rgba(255,255,255,.065);
  --zs2-sel:    rgba(255,255,255,.215);
  --zs2-well:   rgba(0,0,0,.34);
  --zs2-well-pg: rgba(255,255,255,.035);  /* black on near-black is nothing,
                                             so on --bg a well goes UP        */

  /* The seam between two rows inside one card. A machined panel has a CUT,
     not a drawn line: dark on top, one sliver of bounce underneath. The step
     across the pair is 1.38:1 where a single hairline manages 1.26:1 — small
     numbers, but it is the whole difference between reading the 22-row
     Keybinds wall and scanning it. Deliberately quiet: the card's own edge
     does the grouping, the seam only stops two rows touching. */
  --zs2-cut:    rgba(0,0,0,.50);
  --zs2-bounce: rgba(255,255,255,.055);

  --zs2-sheen:  inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.075);  /* light on a top edge */
  --zs2-lit:    inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.14);   /* …on a raised key    */
  --zs2-groove: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.55), inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.06);

  /* The side wall of a key: the 2px of plastic you can see between the cap
     and the board. It collapses to nothing on press, which is what makes a
     button read as pressABLE before it is touched — the thing a flat
     rectangle of slightly different grey can never say. */
  --zs2-wall:   0 2px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.62);
  --zs2-sunk:   inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.55);

  /* Ion surfaces, RE-MEASURED on the new card (#21272f):
       tint .14   → #234045 · accent on it 6.31:1 · --text on it 9.65:1
       line .55   → #298986 · 3.59:1 vs its own card
       dash .45   → #1d7c79 · 3.02:1 vs the page ground                     */
  --zs2-ion-tint: rgba(111, 155, 255,.14);
  --zs2-ion-line: rgba(111, 155, 255,.55);
  --zs2-ion-dash: rgba(111, 155, 255,.45);

  /* The side wall of the ONE solid-accent cap in the app. Hand-derived from
     --accent rather than computed, because there is no way to darken a custom
     property inside a box-shadow that is safe on every engine this ships to.
     IF --accent EVER MOVES, MOVE THIS TOO. It moved on 2026-08-28 when the
     cap stopped being cyan→blue→teal and became a ramp of the accent itself
     (phone.html :root --cap-*): #123a6b measures 3.27:1 against the cap's new
     bottom stop #5b86e8, which is what makes the wall visible as a wall. */
  --zs2-ion-wall: #123a6b;

  /* The second ink. The app measured 44–90% one single grey by character
     count, which is most of why it read as a settings list: when everything
     is secondary, nothing is primary. This is the middle level — bright
     enough to be content, dim enough to sit under a title.
       #b9c7d7 on a card 8.75:1 · on a control face 6.96:1 · on --bg 11.45:1
       · on a well 11.25:1 · on a selected pill 5.43:1
     --muted stays, demoted to true metadata: counts, units, timestamps. It
     lives in phone.html's :root and went to #93a1b4 in the same edit that
     raised the control face — see §1.5. The two inks stay 1.53:1 apart, so
     they remain distinguishable from each other, not only from the page. */
  --zs-ink-2: #b9c7d7;
}


/* ══ 2. TYPE, APPLIED ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The inversion fix is the whole of this section. The primary label of every
   settings row was 14.5px/400 — the same weight as the paragraph in the note
   underneath it — while the uppercase header introducing it was 12px/700.
   Row titles go to 15.5px/600 in --text and headers drop to 11.5px muted, so
   size and colour now outrank weight and the order is right way up. */

.hero .nm {
  font-size: var(--fs-hero); font-weight: var(--fw-hero);
  line-height: var(--lh-hero); letter-spacing: var(--tr-hero);
}
/* phone.html already puts white-space:nowrap + ellipsis on .phead h2 and on
   .hero .nm, so 26px and 19px cannot wrap a long config name — they clip,
   which is what the ellipsis is for. Verified in the markup before sizing up. */
.phead h2, .zs-gate h2, .sheet .shead h3, .aimph b {
  font-size: var(--fs-title); font-weight: var(--fw-title);
  line-height: var(--lh-title); letter-spacing: var(--tr-t);
}
.appbar h1 { font-size: 16.5px; letter-spacing: var(--tr-l); }

.cfg .cn .name, .smtitle {
  font-size: var(--fs-lead); font-weight: var(--fw-lead);
  line-height: var(--lh-lead); letter-spacing: var(--tr-l);
}

/* THE ROW TITLE — the most common line of text in the product. One rule for
   a settings row on Home and a settings row in the editor, because they are
   the same object and used not to look like it. .flab is a DIV in the
   renderer (fld() builds `<div class="flab">`), so this lands on the whole
   label block and the <em> under it is re-quietened below. */
.drow .t .lab, .item .t .lab, .flab, .acc > .ah, .lrow .src, .salab {
  font-size: var(--fs-row); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  line-height: var(--lh-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r); color: var(--text);
}
.lrow .src { font-size: 14.5px; }
.card .cn  { font-size: 14px; font-weight: var(--fw-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r); }

/* Secondary lines. ONE size for every explanatory string in the app — hints,
   notes, sub-labels, leads, card descriptions — because they are the same
   kind of sentence and were previously six sizes between 11.5 and 13.5px. */
.drow .t .hint, .item .t .hint, .flab em, .note, .lead, .card .cd,
.hero .live, .idline .mt, .phead .psub, .smsub, .smcap, .pkr .pkn em,
.acc > .ab .lead, .ph, .sahcap, .satnote, .mechcurve span {
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-sub);
  line-height: var(--lh-sub); letter-spacing: var(--tr-s);
}

/* THE EYEBROW. 11.5px/700 uppercase at --muted: 6.45:1 on --bg, 5.24:1 on a
   card — both past AA, and unmistakably subordinate to a 15.5px/600 title. */
.grp, .sh, .pkh {
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow); font-weight: var(--fw-eyebrow);
  line-height: var(--lh-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--tr-e);
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
}

/* THE READOUTS. Mono + tabular, and only here — see the --zs-mono comment for
   why .stat b and .smval are excluded by name. */
.flab .rv, .stp .sv, .stp input.svedit {
  font-family: var(--zs-mono);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  font-feature-settings: "tnum" 1, "zero" 1;
  letter-spacing: -.01em;
}
/* .stat b still gets tabular figures — a number that ticks 59→60 must not
   shove its row sideways — it just does not get the mono FACE, because half
   of them are sentences. */
.stat b, .dvb-meta b, .lrow .rt b {
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
/* The ● that promises "this saves to your board". phone.html paints its
   COLOUR at runtime from the live link state — that is a signal, so this only
   sizes and spaces it. */
.livedot { font-size: 8px; vertical-align: 2px; margin-left: 6px; }


/* ══ 3. CHROME — the two header bars ══════════════════════════════════════
   zs-brand.css already makes these glass and fades their lower boundary
   instead of ruling it. All that is missing is a lit top edge and enough room
   for 19px type. */

.appbar, .phead {
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  padding-left: var(--sp-3); padding-right: var(--sp-3);
}
.phead { padding-bottom: 13px; gap: var(--sp-2); }
.phead .psub { color: var(--muted); margin-top: 2px; }

/* ‹ Home had a 44px hit area and no visible edges, so on a phone it read as a
   label rather than a control. It becomes a chip: same target, obviously
   pressable, and the negative margins keep the header's optical alignment
   exactly where it was.  --accent on the control face: 8.55:1. */
.pback {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center;
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 13px;
  margin: -2px var(--sp-1) -2px -4px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);        /* 3.26:1 inward — see .cogbtn */
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
}
.pback:active { filter: none; border-color: var(--accent); }
.appbar .cogbtn {
  width: 44px; height: 44px; min-height: 44px;
  padding: 0; margin: 0 -4px 0 0; flex: none;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge); border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);                  /* 8.75:1 on that face */
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
}
.appbar .cogbtn svg { width: 21px; height: 21px; }
/* The logo chip was rgba(88,166,255,…) — the PlayStation family colour spent
   as brand furniture, which rule 6 forbids in both directions. */
.appbar .logochip {
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border-color: var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);
}


/* ══ 4. SECTION HEADERS — .grp, .sh, .pkh ═════════════════════════════════
   These three were pixel-identical and yet collapsed differently against
   their siblings, which is the entire reason phone.html carries
   `.sh+.grp-card,.sh+.acc,…{margin-top:0}` at line 366. One component now,
   one rhythm: --sp-4 above, --sp-2 below, and the space under a header
   belongs to the header (§13 restates the adjacencies so nothing below adds
   its own).

   THE BRACKET IS THE RETICLE'S CORNER — 9px, two 2px arms. It is identical on
   every header, carries no state, and is what makes eight consecutive
   sections read as eight sections instead of one long run. It is also the
   only piece of the actual mark that appears anywhere between the app bar and
   the hero.  --accent on the ground: 11.20:1. */
.grp, .sh, .pkh {
  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--sp-2);
}
.grp::before, .sh::before, .pkh::before {
  content: ""; flex: none;
  width: 9px; height: 9px;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
  border-top: 2px solid var(--accent);
  border-top-left-radius: 1px;
}
/* Only .sh and .pkh get the trailing rule. .grp can carry a .gact pinned
   right with margin-left:auto, and a flex-grow pseudo-element would land
   AFTER it in flex order and shove it inward. Verified against phone.html
   1333 and 1346, which are the only two .gact call sites. */
.sh::after, .pkh::after {
  content: ""; flex: 1 1 auto; height: 1px; min-width: 12px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--line), transparent);
}
.grp { padding: var(--sp-4) 2px var(--sp-2); }
.sh  { margin: var(--sp-4) 2px var(--sp-2); }
.pkh { margin: var(--sp-3) 2px var(--sp-2); }
/* Restated at (0,2,0) to beat phone.html:253. It is dead today — renderEdMenu
   emits five .item rows and no headers at all — but it is one line, and the
   day a header is added to that screen it would otherwise sit at an 18px
   indent while its rows sit at 14px. */
.menu .grp { padding: var(--sp-4) 4px var(--sp-2); }

/* THE HEADER'S TRAILING ACTION, SPLIT ON THE TAG.
   Since 2026-08-18 renderHome emits `<button class="gact" id="livechip">` —
   the chip DOES something now (it opens the board-facts sheet), so it wears
   the pressable chip treatment below alongside the Configs screen's
   `<button class="gact" id="orderbtn">Reorder</button>`. Its green/amber
   state colour is written inline by renderHome/setOnline and out-ranks the
   accent here, which is exactly right: the state is the signal, the border
   and fill only say "tappable". The span.gact rule stays for any future
   read-only report a header carries. */
.grp button.gact {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 12px; margin-right: -4px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);        /* a KEY's edge, not a card's:
                                              --line reads 2.36:1 on this
                                              fill and misses 1.4.11        */
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}
.grp span.gact {
  padding: 5px 10px; margin-right: -2px;
  border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background: var(--zs2-well-pg);
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .10em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}

/* The first thing on a screen should not be pushed down by a full section
   gap. (0,2,0) to match phone.html's `.dbody>:first-child`. */
.dbody > :first-child, .home > :first-child, .menu > :first-child { margin-top: 4px; }
.dbody > .sh:first-child { padding-top: 2px; }


/* ══ 5. SURFACES ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Every box in the app is now exactly one of four materials. The card
   gradient runs light→dark top to bottom; that is not a flourish, it is the
   cheapest possible way to say "a physical panel lit from above", and it is
   why the card no longer needs a loud 3:1 outline to be seen. */

.hero, .cfg, .drow, .item, .grp-card, .acc, .card, .tile, .mechcurve,
.sastripbox, .sacat {                    /* last two: beats the runtime block */
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen), var(--el-1);
}
.grp-card, .acc {
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* ── 5.1 THE HERO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The most important element on the home screen differed from a plain config
   row by 1px of type and a box-shadow that composited to 1.02:1 against the
   ground — mathematically invisible. It is now the only surface in the app
   carrying real elevation, an ion sheen AND the engraved mark, so it is
   different in KIND rather than in degree.

   RULE 7. renderHome writes `style="border-left-color:<family>"` inline. The
   `border` SHORTHAND is therefore forbidden here — it would reset
   border-left-width to 1px and leave a 5px signal painting a hairline.
   Longhands only, and border-left-color is never named. Same on .cfg. */
.hero {
  border-style: solid;
  border-width: 1px 1px 1px 5px;
  border-top-color: var(--line);
  border-right-color: var(--line);
  border-bottom-color: var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-rail);
  box-shadow: var(--el-3), var(--zs2-lit);
  overflow: hidden;
  position: relative;
}
.hero > * { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
.hero .top { padding: var(--sp-3); gap: var(--sp-3); align-items: flex-start; }
.hero .art, .hero .top > .art { border-radius: 14px; }
.hero .nm { margin-bottom: 2px; }

/* THE SHEEN — the premium half. A console card is lit from somewhere; this is
   that light.
   THE ALPHA IS CAPPED AT .07 AND THE CAP IS A CONTRAST DECISION. The hero
   carries --muted text (.idline .mt and .hero .live). At the .11 two of the
   four directions proposed, --muted on the sheen measures 4.17:1 and FAILS
   AA. At .07 it is 4.55:1 and passes; --text is 12.08:1 and --accent 7.90:1.
   Nobody who proposed this sheen measured what was underneath it. */
.hero::before {
  content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 0; pointer-events: none;
  background: radial-gradient(130% 85% at 8% -20%, rgba(111, 155, 255,.07), transparent 62%);
}

/* THE MARK — the restraint half. The reticle from the logo, engraved into the
   card's top-right corner and clipped by its radius.

   ENGRAVED, NOT GLOWING, and that is contrast rather than taste. A cyan
   watermark at 13% under the config name drops --muted on top of it to
   3.97:1 and fails; black at 32% RAISES it — --muted goes 5.24 → 5.89 and
   --text 13.91 → 15.63. A darker mark can never hurt legibility on a dark
   card, so it can sit where a lighter one could not. Even stacked on the
   sheen at its brightest, --muted holds 5.45:1. */
.hero::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; z-index: 0; pointer-events: none;
  right: -34px; top: -30px; width: 172px; height: 172px;
  --ret: rgba(0,0,0,.32);
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, transparent 0 43%, var(--ret) 43% 45.4%, transparent 45.9%),
    radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, transparent 0 13%, var(--ret) 13% 15%, transparent 15.5%),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret));
  background-size: 100% 100%, 100% 100%, 2px 17%, 2px 17%, 17% 2px, 17% 2px;
  background-position: 0 0, 0 0, 50% 6%, 50% 94%, 6% 50%, 94% 50%;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
}

/* THE BOARD LINE — offline guidance only, since 2026-08-18. The online
   narration ("Board connected · sending inputs · up 103 min") left the hero
   for the board-facts sheet behind the ● board chip (owner: "that is a
   debug fact"); what renders here now is ONLY the amber can't-reach
   guidance, which is help, not telemetry. Same recessed strip as before,
   so the offline state still reads as a deliberate surface. */
.hero .live {
  margin: 0 var(--sp-3) 14px;
  padding: 10px 12px;
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  color: var(--muted);                     /* 5.94:1 on that well */
}
.hero .live .dot { color: var(--green); font-size: 14px; }

/* "Edit this config" is THE action on HOME, and it was invisible — a faint
   accent label on a grey rail (owner, 2026-08-17: "edit this config is too
   hard to see, and not one colour"). It is now the second sanctioned solid-
   ion surface (§7 owns the first, and the two make the same statement:
   press this and the thing happens). One colour, full width, welded to the
   hero's foot, with .btn.primary's own cap physics and its measured pairs —
   --accent-ink on the three stops 10.77 / 9.75 / 7.91:1, AA everywhere on
   the face. The chevron rides along so it still reads as a door. */
.hero .go {
  min-height: 58px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.38);
  border-radius: 0;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 52%, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 100%);
  background-color: var(--accent);
  color: var(--accent-ink);
  font-size: 15.5px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
  text-shadow: 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.18);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.42), inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.22);
}
.hero .go > span:last-child { font-size: 20px; opacity: .85; }
.hero .go:active, .hero .go.zs-pressing {
  filter: none;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 100%);
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.28);
}

/* ── 5.2 CONFIG ROWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Longhand borders again — the inline border-left-color is a signal. */
.cfg {
  border-style: solid;
  border-width: 1px 1px 1px 5px;
  border-top-color: var(--line);
  border-right-color: var(--line);
  border-bottom-color: var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-rail);
  min-height: 88px;
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
  padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-2) var(--sp-2) 14px;
  gap: 14px;
}
body.a11y-big .cfg { min-height: 96px; }
.cfg .art { border-radius: 14px; }
.cfg .cn .name { color: var(--text); }
.idline { margin-top: 4px; gap: 7px; }
.idline .mt { color: var(--muted); }        /* 5.24:1 on the card */

/* The live row. JS already writes aria-current="true" on it — free state, no
   new class. A brighter face and a defined edge, so it is a different object
   rather than the same rectangle with a tick bolted on. */
.cfg[aria-current="true"] {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cur-a), var(--cur-b));
  border-top-color: var(--zs-edge);
  border-right-color: var(--zs-edge);
  border-bottom-color: var(--zs-edge);
}

/* ✓ / Playing / now.
   VERIFIED AGAINST THE MARKUP, AND THE MARKUP OVERRULES ONE OF THE GRAFTS I
   was handed. renderHome emits
     <span class="cstate"><span class="tick">✓</span>Playing<br>now</span>
   — with a literal <br>. So this block can never be one line, and turning it
   into a horizontal pill (as one direction proposed) would produce a pill
   with a forced line break inside it. It stays a narrow stacked badge, which
   is what the markup is actually shaped like; what it gains is a wash, a real
   border and type that is not 11px/700 grey.
     #56d364 on the green wash 6.44:1 · --green border vs the card 6.30:1
   Colour is never alone here: the tick AND the words are both present. */
.cstate {
  flex: none; width: 62px;
  padding: 5px 4px 6px;
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background: rgba(63,185,80,.15);
  border: 1px solid var(--green);
  color: var(--live-ink);
  font-size: var(--fs-micro); font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .05em; text-transform: uppercase; line-height: 1.25;
}
.cstate .tick { font-size: 15px; letter-spacing: 0; }

/* ── 5.3 NAVIGATION ROWS — .drow and .item ───────────────────────────────
   These were the same content model rendered two ways: a card on Home, a bare
   list line in the editor. The editor menu — the second screen every user
   reaches, and the product's entire feature set — was five undecorated rows
   on the raw ground: no cards, no borders, one radius, 80% grey text. It is
   the most literal case of "this doesn't even look cool".

   One component now. .item supplies its own horizontal margin because .menu
   pads `8px 0` and there is no wrapper element the renderer emits — the row
   provides its own gutter, which needs no markup change at all. */
.drow, .item {
  display: flex; align-items: center;
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  min-height: 74px;
  padding: 14px var(--sp-3);
  gap: 14px;
}
.drow { margin-top: var(--sp-2); }
.item { margin: var(--sp-2) 14px; }
/* phone.html gives .item a bottom hairline that belonged to the flat list.
   Named explicitly rather than left to the shorthand above, so it is obvious
   this was deliberate and not an accident of ordering. */
.item { border-bottom-color: var(--line); }
body.a11y-big .drow, body.a11y-big .item { min-height: 82px; }
.drow:active, .item:active, .drow.zs-pressing, .item.zs-pressing {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  border-color: var(--zs-edge);
}

/* THE ICON CONTAINER. It existed, it was 30px, it was --muted on --panel2,
   and it appeared on seven rows in the entire app. At 42px it is the cheapest
   scanning aid available — shape first, so it works with colour off.

   GEOMETRY ONLY. `.item .ico.ico-*` at phone.html:589 is (0,3,0) and already
   ships five per-section hues (Overview ion, Aim gold, Keybinds steel, Smart
   Actions orange, Computer Vision the brand gradient). This rule is (0,2,0),
   so width / height / border-radius land everywhere and background /
   border-color / color lose on all five editor rows — which is correct, and
   is why background and colour are set on `.drow .ico` alone. renderEdMenu
   emits `class="ico ico-<id>"` on every single row, and renderHome's two
   device rows emit a bare `class="ico"`; both verified in source.

   Re-measured on THIS file's card fill, because the five hues were tuned
   against the old --panel and a lighter card could have broken them:
     Overview  ion   6.89:1   Aim    gold  6.51:1
     Keybinds  steel 7.46:1   Smart  orange 5.92:1
   All four past AA. (Computer Vision is a gradient border, not a glyph
   colour, so there is no single pair to state.) */
.drow .ico, .item .ico {
  width: 42px; height: 42px; flex: none;
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
}
.drow .ico svg, .item .ico svg { width: 21px; height: 21px; }
.drow .ico {
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);                    /* 6.70:1 on its own tint;
                                              the edge 3.72:1 vs the card */
}
/* .item.gated is dead code today — no entry in phone.html's SECTIONS array
   carries a `gate:` property, so gateOK() always returns true and the class
   is never emitted. It is fixed anyway because it costs one line and it is
   wrong: phone.html:225 paints it in rgba(88,166,255,…), the PlayStation
   family colour, as generic chrome. The :not() guard is what stops this from
   ever overwriting one of the five section hues. */
.item.gated .ico:not([class*="ico-"]) {
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border-color: var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);
}
.chev { color: var(--muted); font-size: 17px; flex: none; }
.item:active .chev, .drow:active .chev, .cfg:active .chev { color: var(--accent); }

/* ── 5.4 CARDS, TILES, PLACEHOLDERS ─────────────────────────────────────── */
.cards { gap: var(--sp-2); }
.card {
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  padding: 14px var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3);
  display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 118px;
}
/* The icon gets a ruled shelf, so a 31-card grid has a horizon to scan along
   instead of 31 free-floating blobs. .card.satpl carries an inline
   `border-left:3px solid <family>` from smart-actions.js — inline beats this
   file, so that stripe survives untouched. Verified at smart-actions.js:3374. */
.card .ci {
  color: var(--accent);                    /* 9.10:1 on the card */
  padding-bottom: var(--sp-2);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--zs2-cut);
  box-shadow: 0 1px 0 var(--zs2-bounce);
}
.card .ci svg { width: 24px; height: 24px; }
.card .cn { margin-top: var(--sp-2); color: var(--text); }
.card .cd { color: var(--muted); margin-top: var(--sp-1); }
.card:active, .card.zs-pressing {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  border-color: var(--zs-edge);
}

.tile {
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-2);
  position: relative;
}
.tile .tart { height: 48px; }
/* Selected: a 2px edge (a WIDTH change, readable with colour off), an ion
   wash, and a tick in the corner. Three channels, per rule 6. */
.tile.on {
  border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--accent);
  padding: 15px 9px;                       /* hold the box the same size */
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ion-tint), rgba(111, 155, 255,.06));
}
.tile.on::after {
  content: "✓"; position: absolute; top: 6px; right: 9px;
  font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent);
}

/* "Nothing here yet" should not look like a finished surface — dashed stays,
   but on a recess rather than a fake card. */
.ph {
  background: var(--zs2-well-pg);
  border: 1px dashed var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  padding: var(--sp-3);
  color: var(--muted);                     /* 5.88:1 on that well */
}
.mechcurve { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r-card); padding: var(--sp-3); }
.mechcurve span { color: var(--zs-ink-2); }
.art { border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl); background: var(--zs2-well-pg); }
.art.photo { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(255,255,255,.18); }

/* ── THE NO-PHOTO ART TILE (.art.mkart) ──────────────────────────────────
   Owner, 2026-08-17: "the picture for configs needs to be better" — with no
   photo the tiles were small dark squares. artHTML (phone.html) now emits a
   COMPOSED tile: the config's own colour (--cfg, inline on the span — the
   same colour the left stripe and the pad's light bar carry, so it is
   config identity, never a family badge) becomes a real backdrop; the
   family mark floats on it at 70% with a shadow instead of wallpapering the
   whole square; and a game-locked config engraves its game's initial behind
   the mark on tiles big enough to hold it (the hero). Everything here is
   decorative — the idline text beside the tile is the accessible identity.

   color-mix DEGRADES SAFELY: if the engine lacks it the background-image
   declaration is invalid and drops, leaving the flat --panel2 tile —
   exactly yesterday's look, never a broken one. */
.art.mkart {
  position: relative;
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--panel2);
  background-image: radial-gradient(130% 130% at 22% 14%,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--cfg, #4a5568) 55%, #0d1118),
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--cfg, #4a5568) 20%, #0d1118) 62%,
    #0a0e14);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(255,255,255,.10),
              inset 0 -8px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.30);
}
.art.mkart .mkbadge {
  position: relative; z-index: 1;
  width: 70%; height: 70%; display: block;
  filter: drop-shadow(0 3px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.5));
}
/* phone.html's `.art svg{width:100%;height:100%}` already fills the badge
   into this wrapper; restated here so the tile never depends on rule order. */
.art.mkart .mkbadge svg { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: block; }
/* the engraved game initial: behind the badge, clipped by the tile, and at
   an alpha that can only ever be texture — never a second glyph competing
   with the mark. font-size arrives inline from artHTML (92% of the tile). */
.art.mkart .gm0 {
  position: absolute; z-index: 0;
  right: -3%; bottom: -16%;
  font-style: normal; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1;
  letter-spacing: -.04em;
  color: rgba(255,255,255,.15);
  pointer-events: none; user-select: none;
}


/* ══ 6. ROWS INSIDE A CARD ════════════════════════════════════════════════
   .fld is the workhorse — every toggle, slider, stepper and dropdown in the
   product. The row gets a real height so a 44px control has somewhere to
   live, and the divider becomes a machined seam: a dark cut with a light
   bounce under it, which steps 1.38:1 across the pair against the 1.26:1 a
   single hairline manages. */
.fld {
  padding: 13px var(--sp-3);
  min-height: 60px; gap: 14px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--zs2-cut);
  box-shadow: 0 1px 0 var(--zs2-bounce);
}
.fld:last-child { border-bottom: none; box-shadow: none; }
.fld.col { padding-top: 14px; padding-bottom: var(--sp-3); }
.fld.col .rng { margin-top: 12px; }
.flab em {
  display: block; font-style: normal; margin-top: 3px;
  color: var(--muted); font-weight: var(--fw-sub);   /* 5.24:1 on the card */
}
.flab .rv { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; font-size: 13.5px; }  /* 9.10:1 */
.fld.tapper:active, .fld.tapper.zs-pressing { background-color: var(--press-wash); }
.grp-card + .grp-card { margin-top: var(--sp-2); }

/* List rows and stat rows keep their border-TOP direction (phone.html's
   :first-child rules depend on it), so the bounce goes INSIDE at the top —
   dark cut, then the light catching the lower lip of the groove. Same
   physics, opposite geometry. */
.lrow {
  padding: 13px 0; gap: var(--sp-2); min-height: 54px;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--zs2-cut);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--zs2-bounce);
}
.stat {
  padding: 12px 0; gap: var(--sp-2);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--zs2-cut);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--zs2-bounce);
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); color: var(--zs-ink-2);   /* 9.31:1 on the card */
}
.lrow:first-child, .stat:first-child { box-shadow: none; }
.grp-card > .stat, .grp-card > .lrow { padding-left: var(--sp-3); padding-right: var(--sp-3); }
/* Every stat value used to be ion. Accent on 71 read-only numbers is accent
   meaning nothing — it is the same colour as the primary button. --text/700
   (13.91:1); only a genuinely good state stays green (6.30:1). */
.stat b { color: var(--text); font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: 700; text-align: right; }
.stat b.ok, .stat .ok { color: var(--green); }
.lrow .arrow { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; opacity: .8; }
.lrow .rt { gap: var(--sp-2); }


/* ══ 7. BUTTONS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The owner named these twice, so they get the most work in the file. The
   second time, verbatim: "the background and the buttons are all the same
   color" and "obviously button clicks is too much of the same colors".

   A button is a KEY: a face lit from above, a defined edge at 3.26:1 against
   its own fill and 5.37:1 against the page, 2px of visible side wall, and
   52px of height. On press the wall collapses and the face sinks. Nothing
   here is decoration for its own sake — the wall is what makes a rectangle
   read as pressABLE before it is touched, and `filter:brightness()` (what the
   kit used) cannot express any of it: it just makes the same flat rectangle
   paler, which says "this got hotter", the wrong metaphor for a key going
   down.

   ── FIVE ROLES, ZERO RENAMES (2026-08-18) ────────────────────────────────
   Every class below already existed. What is new is that each one now says
   WHICH KIND OF ACTION it is, and each is measured against ITS OWN FILL —
   not against the page — because the fill is the adjacency 1.4.11 cares
   about and the one every earlier pass skipped.

     1 PRIMARY      .btn.primary, .hero .go, and the ONE create control that
                    is a screen's whole first move (zs-fixes §11's ＋ New
                    config, §17's ＋ Add a button on Keybinds)
                    "Press this and the thing happens." Solid ion. The census
                    is short on purpose and every addition to it is argued
                    where it is made — the RULE is that a cyan slab makes
                    that one statement and nothing else ever borrows the
                    appearance. Never tinted (§17), never dimmed.
     2 SECONDARY    .btn, .ib, .pbtn, .pback, .grp button.gact, .mechpill,
                    .stp button, .appbar .cogbtn, .sheet .shead .sclose,
                    .pk, .pkr
                    "Available, and it is a key." The control face + edge +
                    wall. This is the role the ladder change is FOR.
     3 DESTRUCTIVE  .btn.danger, .ib.danger
                    "This removes something." Red wash + hazard hatch + red
                    edge, and NO lit top edge — so it reads as a different
                    MATERIAL, not merely a red key.
     4 QUIET        .btn.ghost, .grp span.gact, .chev
                    "Available, but not the move." No fill, no wall, no
                    lift. That absence IS the role.
     5 CREATE       .newcfg, .addbtn
                    An empty socket. Recessed, dashed, ion glyph.

   NOT A BUTTON ROLE: .seg2/.subtabs/#edtabs `.on`. That is SELECTION and it
   lives in §8. It must never take a solid ion fill — that is the whole
   reason §8 exists.

   The shear runs across every one of them, so the control layer has a
   silhouette of its own that the card layer does not. */

.btn {
  min-height: 52px;
  padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: var(--sp-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text);                      /* 10.41:1 on the face's top stop,
                                              13.53:1 on its bottom          */
  font-size: 15px; font-weight: var(--fw-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), var(--zs2-wall), var(--el-1);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
}
/* Small still means tappable. 44px is the floor, not a suggestion — this used
   to render at ~37.6px, which is under the threshold on the one product whose
   entire purpose is lowering physical demand. */
.btn.sm {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  font-size: 13.5px; display: inline-flex; width: auto; margin-top: 0;
}

/* THE SOLID ACCENT SURFACE. After §8, exactly two elements in the app wear
   it: this button and HOME's "Edit this config" rail (§5.1) — tabs and
   segments no longer borrow the appearance, so this face means "press this
   and something happens" and nothing else. Both surfaces make that statement.

   IT USED TO BE CYAN, AND THAT WAS THE APP'S "NOT ONE COLOUR" BUG. The three
   stops were #3ee3d8 → #6f9bff → #26c4ba, so the accent was the middle 52%
   of the loudest object on every screen and the eye read the teal ends. The
   stops are now a light-to-deep ramp of the accent itself (--cap-a/b/c, set
   in phone.html's :root beside --btn-1-*), which is the only change: the lit
   top edge, the hard side wall and the inverted press are untouched.

   The hard `0 3px 0` side wall is the whole trick — it is what reads as a
   physical cap rather than a coloured rectangle, and a flat alpha overlay has
   nothing like it.
     --btn-1-ink #04120f  on the top stop #93b6ff   9.44:1
                          on the mid stop #6f9bff   7.11:1
                          on the low stop #5b86e8   5.48:1
     the wall #123a6b against the cap's own bottom  3.27:1
     the cap's low stop against the page ground     5.20:1
   AA at every point on the face.

   Prefixed with `body` so it beats zs-brand.css:252 AND the runtime blocks. */
body .btn.primary {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 52%, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 100%);
  background-color: var(--accent);
  border-color: var(--btn-1-edge, #a8c4ff);
  color: var(--accent-ink);
  font-weight: 700;
  text-shadow: 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.18);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.42),
              inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,.22),
              0 3px 0 var(--zs2-ion-wall),
              0 8px 20px var(--zs-ion-glow);
}
/* PRESSED. The gradient INVERTS as well as sinking — light comes from above,
   so a cap that has travelled down is lit from a lower angle. The wall is
   gone, which is the part the eye actually reads. */
body .btn.primary:active, body .btn.primary.zs-pressing {
  filter: none;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-c,#5b86e8) 0%, var(--cap-b,#6f9bff) 100%);
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.28), 0 0 14px rgba(111, 155, 255,.16);
}

/* DISABLED — AND THIS IS THE ONE RULE IN §7 THAT IS COUNTER-INTUITIVE.
   NEVER DIM A PRIMARY. The generic `.btn:disabled{opacity:.55}` below drops a
   cyan cap to a washed-out cyan cap: still obviously the loudest thing on the
   screen, still the thing the eye goes to, and now unreadable. A disabled
   primary is not a quieter primary — it is NOT THE PRIMARY RIGHT NOW. So it
   swaps down a whole role, to the SECONDARY face, and keeps full opacity.
     --muted #93a1b4 on that face 4.56:1 — AA, deliberately still readable,
     because "why can't I press this" is answered by the label.
   The wall goes, so it does not read as pressable. aria-disabled is honoured
   as well as :disabled — smart-actions.js uses the attribute form, and a
   role that only styles one of the two is a role with a hole in it. */
body .btn.primary:disabled,
body .btn.primary[aria-disabled="true"] {
  opacity: 1;
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  border-color: var(--zs-edge);
  color: var(--muted);
  text-shadow: none;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* ROLE 3 — DESTRUCTIVE. IT IS A SOLID RED KEY. (2026-08-18)

   WHAT WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS MEASURED ACROSS THE WHOLE APP: forty-one
   captured frames of the running product contained 0.00% of #cf3f45. Not one
   pixel. Every screen. The role token existed in phone.html (--btn-3-bg
   #cf3f45 on #ffffff), the renderers tagged eleven controls `.btn.danger` and
   three `.ib.danger` — and this rule, loading later, threw the fill away and
   painted a 10%-alpha wash that composites to #372d33 on a card. So "Factory
   reset — erase phone-app data" and "Delete this config" rendered as GREY
   KEYS WITH A RED OUTLINE, indistinguishable at arm's length from "Restart
   adapter" beside them. A destructive control you have to read to identify
   is not a destructive control.

   The wash was defensible when it was written — it made red a WARNING rather
   than an invitation — but it optimised the wrong risk. armConfirm() already
   makes every one of these a two-tap control; the first tap cannot destroy
   anything. Nothing here is a hair trigger, so the fill is free to be loud,
   and loud is what "this removes something" needs to be from across a room.

   COLOUR IS STILL NEVER THE ONLY SIGNAL, and all three other channels are
   kept intact — the hatch, the missing lit edge, and the words:
     · the 4px hazard hatch stays, inverted. It was light-red-on-dark; it is
       now black-on-red, so it still reads with the colour channel removed
       and still says "hazard tape" rather than "button".
     · NO lit top edge, where every other key has one, so it is a different
       MATERIAL and not merely a red key.
     · armConfirm() still rewrites the label to "Tap again to confirm".
   MEASURED on the solid fill:
     #ffffff ink on #cf3f45                4.72:1  (AA)
     #ffffff ink on the hatch's dark band  6.03:1
     the fill against a card               3.86:1  (1.4.11 wants 3:1)
     the fill against the page             4.25:1
     the #ff8078 edge against the fill     1.61:1 — decorative only, which is
       fine now: the FILL identifies the control, so the border no longer has
       to carry 1.4.11 on its own the way an outlined ghost's did.
   #e5484d (--red) is NOT used as the fill: with white it measures 3.91:1 and
   misses AA on a 15px label. It stays where it is ink on dark. */
.btn.danger {
  background-color: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45);
  background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(-45deg,
      rgba(0,0,0,.16) 0 4px, transparent 4px 9px);
  border-color: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45);
  color: var(--btn-3-ink, #ffffff);
  font-weight: 700;
  text-shadow: none;
  box-shadow: 0 3px 0 #7e2429, 0 8px 20px rgba(207,63,69,.26);
}
/* PRESSED: the wall goes and the face darkens — the same physics as the
   primary's, so the two loud roles behave identically under a finger. */
.btn.danger:active, .btn.danger.zs-pressing {
  filter: none;
  background-color: var(--btn-3-press);
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.42);
}
/* …and once armConfirm() has armed it, the key goes brighter still. The
   label already changed; this is the second channel for the same fact. */
.btn.danger[data-armed="1"] {
  background-color: var(--red);
  box-shadow: 0 3px 0 #7e2429, 0 0 0 2px rgba(255,128,120,.55),
              0 8px 22px rgba(229,72,77,.34);
}

/* ROLE 3b — CAUTION. THE OLD DESTRUCTIVE FACE, KEPT, WITH A JOB OF ITS OWN.
   Solid red now means "this removes something". A handful of controls are
   serious but REVERSIBLE — "Restart board agent" drops your link for a few
   seconds and gives it back — and calling those the same colour as a factory
   reset is the exact defect this pass is fixing, in the other direction. So
   they get the treatment `.btn.danger` used to have: red edge, red ink,
   hazard hatch, NO fill. Read left to right the ladder is now legible:
     solid ion = do the thing · solid red = destroy the thing ·
     red outline = it will interrupt you · grey key = ordinary.
     #ff8078 on the wash 7.31:1 page · 5.43:1 card
     the --red border vs the page 6.70:1 · vs a card 5.12:1 */
.btn.caution {
  background-color: rgba(255,98,89,.10);
  background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(-45deg,
      rgba(255,98,89,.09) 0 4px, transparent 4px 9px);
  border-color: var(--red);
  color: var(--red-ink);
  text-shadow: none;
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-wall), var(--el-1);
}
.btn.caution:active, .btn.caution.zs-pressing {
  filter: none; background-color: rgba(255,98,89,.17); box-shadow: var(--zs2-sunk);
}

/* THE SMALL DESTRUCTIVE KEY — the ✕ that deletes a step, a target, a
   shortcut. It is the same role at 44px, and it exists as a CLASS because
   today it is eight inline `style="color:var(--red)"` strings: a role
   expressed as an inline colour cannot be restyled, cannot be measured, and
   cannot follow the ladder when the ladder moves. One modifier on an existing
   class, so nothing renames and the class API is unchanged.
     #ff8078 on the wash over a card 5.43:1 · --red edge vs that wash 4.51:1 */
/* GEOMETRY IS DELIBERATELY NOT RESTATED: .ib is already a 38px cap with a
   48px hit box via phone.html's ::after expander, so the target is legal and
   a 44px cap here would make the ✕ visibly larger than the ↑ ↓ beside it —
   an inconsistency, not a fix. Role changes the MATERIAL, never the size. */
/* …AND IT IS SOLID TOO, for the same measured reason. Global Settings /
   General carries three of these — the ✕ that removes a Navigate-Mode
   shortcut — and every one of them painted #28313f, i.e. the ordinary
   secondary key. The DOM said danger and the pixels said "any other button".
     #ffffff on #cf3f45 4.72:1 · the fill vs a card 3.86:1 */
.ib.danger {
  background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(-45deg,
      rgba(0,0,0,.16) 0 4px, transparent 4px 9px);
  background-color: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45);
  border-color: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45);
  color: var(--btn-3-ink, #ffffff);
  box-shadow: 0 2px 0 #7e2429;
}
.ib.danger:active, .ib.danger.zs-pressing {
  background-color: var(--btn-3-press); box-shadow: inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.42);
}
/* The token re-point two rules below (`.lrow .rt .ib { --red:#ff8078 }`) is
   for an .ib that asks for --red as INK on a dark face. A solid red .ib does
   not, and must not inherit a pink ✕ on a red ground — 1.63:1. Inside a
   SOLID red key the token that means "danger ink" is the white. */
.lrow .rt .ib.danger { --red: var(--btn-3-ink, #ffffff); }
/* …and the call sites that still carry the pre-class inline colour get the
   right ink WITHOUT anyone fighting an inline declaration with !important.
   --red #ff6259 measures 4.07:1 on the new #32373f key face — a fail the
   ladder introduced, on a glyph that deletes things. Re-pointing the TOKEN
   inside this one component is the mechanism CSS provides for exactly this:
   an .ib that never says var(--red) is untouched, and an .ib that does gets
   4.91:1 instead. Delete this rule the day the three call sites take
   .ib.danger (phone-sections.js:5460, smart-actions.js:2304 and :2620). */
.lrow .rt .ib { --red: var(--red-ink); }

/* ROLE 4 — QUIET. The only button that is NOT a key: no wall, no lift. That
   is its whole job — "available, but not the move" — and keeping it flat is
   what lets the raised keys mean something.

   ITS BORDER IS --zs-edge, NOT --line, and the distinction is the whole of
   1.4.11. A ghost has no fill and no elevation, so its BOUNDARY is the only
   thing that identifies it as a control — which puts it in the 3:1 class,
   unlike a card's decorative grouping edge. --line measures 2.96:1 on a card
   and misses; --zs-edge is 4.10:1 there and 5.37:1 on the page. */
.btn.ghost {
  background-image: none; background-color: transparent;
  border-color: var(--zs-edge); box-shadow: none;
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);                  /* 11.45:1 ground · 8.75:1 card */
  font-weight: var(--fw-row);
}
.btn.ghost:active, .btn.ghost.zs-pressing {
  background-color: var(--zs2-well-pg); color: var(--text);
}

/* DISABLED, every role but PRIMARY (which swaps face instead — see above).
   The wall goes first: a key with no side wall does not invite a press. .55
   over the page ground leaves the label at 4.45:1 against its own dimmed
   face — legible but inert, which is what a disabled control should be.
   (WCAG 1.4.3 exempts inactive components from the 4.5:1 floor; this is
   close to it anyway, on purpose, because "greyed out to invisible" is how
   users end up unable to tell a disabled control from a broken screen.)
   aria-disabled is styled alongside :disabled — both forms ship. */
.btn:disabled, .btn[aria-disabled="true"],
.ib:disabled, .ib[aria-disabled="true"],
.pbtn:disabled, .pbtn[aria-disabled="true"] {
  box-shadow: none; opacity: .55;
}

/* PRESS, for every key. The wall collapses into a groove and the face sinks.
   zs-brand.css owns the scale dip and the .zs-pressing class that lets a
   press release when a finger slides off the target; this is only the part
   scale cannot say. `filter:none` cancels phone.html's brightness() so the
   two feedbacks do not fire as two separate events. */
.btn:active, .ib:active, .pbtn:active, .pk:active, .pkr:active,
.stp button:active, .mechpill:active, .sheet .shead .sclose:active,
.appbar .cogbtn:active,
.btn.zs-pressing, .ib.zs-pressing, .pbtn.zs-pressing, .pk.zs-pressing,
.pkr.zs-pressing, .stp button.zs-pressing, .mechpill.zs-pressing {
  filter: none;
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sunk);
}

/* "Add something" — one component, two call sites. These were the same button
   at radius 14 and radius 10. An EMPTY SOCKET: recessed, dashed, ion glyph,
   which is exactly what it means. Dashes at 3.02:1 against the page; a dashed
   line at the old 1.38:1 was a rumour. Ion label at 10.20:1 on the well. */
.newcfg, .addbtn {
  min-height: 54px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  border: 1px dashed var(--zs2-ion-dash);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  background: var(--zs2-well-pg);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
}
.addbtn { margin-top: var(--sp-3); }
.newcfg:active, .addbtn:active {
  filter: none; border-style: solid; border-color: var(--accent);
  background: rgba(111, 155, 255,.10);
}

/* THE SMALL SQUARE KEY. 38px of visible cap, 48px of hit area via the
   ::after expander phone.html already ships — the target is legal without the
   button having to look chunky. */
.ib {
  width: 38px; height: 38px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);                  /* 8.75:1 on the face */
  font-size: 15px; font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), var(--zs2-wall);
}
.ib::after { inset: -5px; }                /* 48px hit target */
.cfg .ib { width: 44px; height: 44px; }

/* ── THE HIT BOXES THAT OVERLAPPED ───────────────────────────────────────
   The ::after expander above is what makes a 38px cap legal, and it is also
   a trap nobody had measured: it puts 5px of INVISIBLE target on every side.
   `.lrow .rt` sets gap:8px, so two adjacent .ib keys have caps 8px apart and
   hit boxes that OVERLAP BY 2px. A tap landing in that seam hit-tests to
   whichever ::after paints last.

   That is not cosmetic. The two keys it happens on are ↑ and ↓ — the Smart
   Actions step reorder (phone-sections.js:5459, smart-actions.js:2302) — so
   a user could press "move up" and get "move down", on precision work, in an
   accessibility product. The author half-knew: phone-sections.js:5460 emits
   `<span style="width:8px">` to buy clearance before the ✕, but nothing
   between the two arrows.

   The arithmetic, stated so nobody "tidies" it back: the row's own 8px flex
   gap PLUS this 10px margin = 18px between caps; minus the 5px of expander
   each side = exactly --sp-1h (8px) of clear air between hit boxes. Scoped
   to .rt so it can only ever affect a row's trailing control cluster. */
.lrow .rt .ib + .ib { margin-left: var(--sp-2); }

/* The ••• that hides five settings behind an unlabelled affordance. CSS
   cannot give it a word, but it can give it a target and an edge you can
   find. */
.mechpill {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 22px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge); border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), var(--zs2-wall);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2); font-size: var(--fs-body); letter-spacing: .3em;
}


/* ══ 8. PICK-ONE-OF-N — .seg2, .subtabs, #edtabs ══════════════════════════
   Four controls doing one job in four geometries, and the SELECTED state of
   all four was byte-identical to .btn.primary. So on a single screen a cyan
   slab meant "which tab you are on", "which mode is selected", "which filter
   is applied" AND "press this to do the thing". This is the change that gives
   cyan its meaning back.

   THE TRACK is a well: the group of options is one recessed object, so the
   choice reads as "one of these", not "four buttons that happen to be
   adjacent".

   THE SELECTED OPTION is a raised neutral pill with an ion rail folded into
   its own box-shadow — no pseudo-element, which is what keeps it out of the
   way of body.a11y-big's padding. Its state is carried on four independent
   channels, which is what rule 6 asks for:
     · label colour   --text 11.04:1 on the pill  vs  --muted 5.94:1 on a
                      track over a card, 5.88:1 over the page
     · weight         700 vs 600
     · elevation      a lit, walled pill vs a flat track, and the pill's own
                      boundary at --zs-edge-hi — 3.14:1 against its top stop,
                      4.49:1 against the track (see §1.4: --zs-edge misses
                      this one adjacency, which is why the token exists)
     · the rail       ion at 7.22:1 on the pill — the ≥3:1 non-text indicator
                      1.4.11 wants, and it is a shape, so greyscale keeps it */

/* THE GAP IS --sp-1h, NOT --sp-1, and it is a touch fix rather than a
   rhythm one. These are 44px-tall buttons sitting 4px apart, edge to edge,
   and .seg2 wraps — so the VERTICAL gap was 4px too, on the control that is
   on nearly every editor screen. 8px is the floor. At 375px a 3-up strip
   still gives ~101px per segment, so no label is under pressure.
   Mitigation already on record: these sit on a recessed track and the
   selected pill is raised with an edge and a 3px rail, so a mis-tap is
   visible immediately. It was still under the guideline. */
.seg2 {
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
  gap: var(--sp-1h); padding: var(--sp-1);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: calc(var(--r-ctl) + 4px);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
}
.seg2 button, .subtabs button, #edtabs button {
  position: relative;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 12px 8px;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  background: none;
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-s);
  box-shadow: none;
}
/* `body` prefix beats zs-brand.css:252 and both runtime blocks.
   2026-08-18 — the selected pill gained a wash of the selection colour over
   its neutral lift (owner: "button clicks is too much of the same colours"
   — ON and OFF were separated by elevation and a 3px rail, which was
   legible but not LOUD). The wash is a fifth channel on top of the four
   documented above, and under a tinted section (§17) it re-inks to the
   section's own hue. --text on the washed pill still measures over 10:1. */
body .seg2 button.on, body .subtabs button.on, body #edtabs button.on {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ion-tint), rgba(111, 155, 255,.04) 72%),
                    linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-sel), var(--zs2-ctl-a));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  border-color: var(--zs-edge-hi);
  color: var(--text);
  font-weight: 700;
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), var(--zs2-wall), inset 0 -3px 0 var(--accent);
}
body .seg2 button.on:active, body .subtabs button.on:active,
body #edtabs button.on:active {
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sunk), inset 0 -3px 0 var(--accent);
}
.seg2 button:active, .subtabs button:active, #edtabs button:active {
  filter: none; color: var(--text);
}

/* THE STICKY SUB-TAB STRIP bleeds to the screen edge (margin:0 -18px) so it
   can occlude content scrolling under it. The TRACK therefore cannot be the
   element's own background, or it would bleed too. ::before draws the well
   inside the bleed.

   position IS NOT TOUCHED. phone.html:431 sets `position:sticky` on .subtabs
   and sticky is already a positioned value, so an absolutely-positioned
   pseudo-element resolves against it correctly. One of the four directions
   wrote `position:relative` here, which would have un-stuck the tab strip on
   every editor screen — a motor-accessibility regression, not a cosmetic one.
   If a future build ever drops the pseudo-element, the strip degrades to
   exactly today's flat row. */
.subtabs {
  padding: var(--sp-2) 18px;
  margin-bottom: var(--sp-2);
  gap: var(--sp-1h);                       /* touch floor — see .seg2 above */
}
.subtabs::before {
  content: ""; position: absolute; z-index: 0;
  left: 18px; right: 18px; top: 6px; bottom: 6px;
  border-radius: calc(var(--r-ctl) + 4px);
  background: var(--zs2-well-pg);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  pointer-events: none;
}
.subtabs button { z-index: 1; }

/* #edtabs is display:none'd by openEditor() — the Essentials/Advanced split
   went away with the tiers. Kept correct rather than deleted so the day it
   comes back it arrives wearing the kit. */
#edtabs {
  gap: var(--sp-1h); padding: var(--sp-2) 14px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
}


/* ══ 9. VALUE CONTROLS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── 9.1 THE TOGGLE, AND THE 44px FIX THAT IS ACTUALLY LEGITIMATE ────────
   .sw renders 52x32 — under the touch-target floor, on the one product whose
   entire purpose is lowering physical demand.

   Inside a .fld this does not bite: phone.html:2033 adds .tapper to the
   enclosing row and forwards the tap, so the real target is the 60px row. But
   smart-actions.js:actionRow emits a .sw inside `.lrow .rt` with NO .fld
   around it, so `el.closest('.fld')` returns null and the 52x32 switch is the
   only target. That is the Smart Actions on/off switch — the most-tapped
   control on that screen.

   BOTH PSEUDO-ELEMENTS ARE SPOKEN FOR: ::after is the knob (phone.html, with
   translateX hardcoded at 22px / 28px, so the geometry cannot move) and
   ::before is zs-micro.css:47's ion bloom, declared `pointer-events:none`.
   Every other direction bolted an ::before expander on top of that and every
   one of them was inert, because none of them loads after zs-micro.

   THIS FILE DOES. So instead of replacing the bloom, it adjusts it: the same
   layer, the same box-shadow, the same opacity-0 resting state and the same
   animation — six more pixels of vertical bleed and pointer-events back on.
   52+10 = 62 wide, 32+12 = 44 tall. A tap anywhere in that box hit-tests to
   the pseudo-element, and a pseudo-element delivers its events to the
   originating element, so .sw's own click handler fires with no JS change.
   The bloom's geometry moves by 1px on the vertical, which is invisible on a
   14px-blurred glow.

   Off and on differ by knob POSITION first, then track fill, then knob
   colour. Position is the channel that survives everything.
     grey knob #8b98a5 on the off well     6.16:1
     accent knob on the on track #223350   4.63:1
     the on track's accent border vs card  6.61:1
   THE KNOB WAS A CYAN→BLUE GRADIENT (#6bf0e6→#6f9bff) until 2026-08-28, i.e.
   "on" was a different colour from the accent that means "on" everywhere
   else. It is a ramp of the accent now, like the cap. */
.sw {
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
}
.sw::before {
  top: -6px; bottom: -6px;                 /* 32 → 44px of touchable height */
  pointer-events: auto;
}
.sw::after { background: linear-gradient(180deg,var(--knob-a),var(--knob-b)); box-shadow: var(--el-1); }
body .sw.on { background: rgba(111, 155, 255,.28); border-color: var(--accent); }
body .sw.on::after {
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff), var(--cap-b,#6f9bff));
  box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--zs-ion-glow), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.5);
}

/* ── 9.2 THE SLIDER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   A milled groove with a knurled cap riding in it. The knurl is a repeating
   gradient, not an image — it costs nothing and it is the most hardware-
   looking pixel in the app, on the one control that is pure drag with no
   label, where an achromatic texture cue is the only extra channel available.

   Three background layers on the input's own background-box, which is exactly
   the mechanism phone.html already used. ::-webkit-slider-runnable-track is
   deliberately NOT used: it would give rounded ends, and a slider that
   silently loses its track in a packaged build is worse than square ends. The
   44px height is the touch target and does not move.
     ion thumb against the groove 10.32:1 */
input[type=range].rng {
  height: 44px; border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background:
    linear-gradient(var(--track-hi), var(--track-hi))
      50% calc(50% + 4px) / 100% 1px no-repeat,
    linear-gradient(180deg, var(--track-a), var(--track-b))
      50% 50% / 100% 8px no-repeat,
    linear-gradient(var(--panel), var(--panel))
      50% 50% / 100% 8px no-repeat;
}
input[type=range].rng::-webkit-slider-thumb {
  -webkit-appearance: none; width: 30px; height: 30px;
  border-radius: 50%; border: none;
  /* the cap it rides was teal (#6bf0e6→#22bdb3) — the one control in the app
     that is pure drag with no label was painted in a colour that meant
     nothing anywhere else. It is the accent's ramp now; the knurl, which is
     the channel that survives colour being removed, is untouched. */
  background:
    repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(0,0,0,.22) 0 1px, transparent 1px 3px),
    linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff), var(--cap-c,#5b86e8));
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--bg), 0 0 0 4px var(--zs2-ion-line),
              0 3px 7px rgba(0,0,0,.65), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.5);
}
input[type=range].rng::-moz-range-thumb {
  width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; border: none;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--cap-a,#93b6ff), var(--cap-c,#5b86e8));
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--bg), 0 3px 7px rgba(0,0,0,.65);
}

/* ── 9.3 THE STEPPER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The most instrument-like control in the kit, and it was a rounded pill with
   two glyphs in it. It becomes literal: a well holding two raised caps, with
   the number engraved in the recess between them and a division line on each
   side of it. The divisions matter directly for a tremor user — they are what
   makes the two keys separately identifiable from the tappable value field.
     ion glyph on a cap 8.55:1 · --text value on the well 15.77:1            */
.stp {
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  gap: 0; padding: 3px; overflow: hidden;
}
.stp button {
  width: 46px; height: 46px;
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), var(--zs2-wall);
  color: var(--accent); font-size: 21px; font-weight: var(--fw-row);
}
.stp button:first-child { margin-right: 1px; border-right: 1px solid var(--zs2-cut); }
.stp button:last-child  { margin-left:  1px; border-left:  1px solid var(--zs2-cut); }
body.a11y-big .stp button { width: 50px; height: 50px; }
.stp .sv {
  min-width: 58px; min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-1);
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text);
}
/* phone.html owns the suffix via content:attr(data-suffix) — this only
   re-inks and un-monos it, because "%" / "ms" / "Hz" are words. */
.stp .sv::after {
  font-family: -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Arial, sans-serif;
  font-size: 11px; font-weight: var(--fw-row); color: var(--zs-ink-2);
  letter-spacing: 0; margin-left: 4px;
}
.stp input.svedit {
  border-radius: var(--r-chip); font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700;
  background: var(--zs2-well); border-color: var(--accent);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
}

/* ── 9.4 SELECTS AND TEXT FIELDS ─────────────────────────────────────────
   Not on the brief's list, but they sit inside .fld on every screen and a
   restyled row with a stock select in it looks broken. You type INTO these,
   so they are wells, not keys. */
select.sel, input.txt {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 12px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  color: var(--text);                      /* 15.77:1 on that well */
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
}
input.txt { padding: 12px 14px; font-weight: var(--fw-body); }
select.sel:focus, input.txt:focus { border-color: var(--accent); }
/* 40px was 4px under the floor on a PRIMARY interaction, and there was room
   for it all along: .lrow is min-height 54px (60 in body.a11y-big), so 44px
   collides with nothing. A select you can miss is a select you set wrong. */
.lrow .sel { min-height: 44px; font-size: var(--fs-sub); }

/* ── 9.5 PROGRESS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Styled in three files and emitted ZERO times — I grepped both renderers.
   It is the only home the brand gradient has, so it is kept correct rather
   than deleted: the first screen that needs a bar gets the house one instead
   of inventing another.  ion against the well: 10.32:1 */
.prog {
  height: 10px; border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
}
.prog > i { background: var(--zs-grad); border-radius: var(--r-pill); }

/* ── 9.6 SWATCHES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Colour IS the content here — the one place hue may stand alone — so the
   selected state cannot be another colour: it is a ring plus a white tick
   with its own shadow, which survives on any swatch value including
   near-white. 36px is under the floor; ::before buys 46px without growing the
   dot, which has to stay small enough to sit six across at 390px.
   ::before and ::after on .sc are both free — full census in the header.

   THE ROW GAP IS 18px, AND IT COSTS A WRAP. .swatches shipped gap:12px, so
   36px dots with 46px hit boxes sat 2px apart — the tightest adjacency in
   the app. 18px puts exactly 8px of clear air between hit boxes.

   Measured in the real container rather than assumed: the six-colour row
   lives in a .grp-card (14px padding, 1px border) inside an accordion .ab
   (14px padding) inside .dbody (18px). At 375px that is 339 − 28 − 2 − 28 =
   281px of content, and 6×36 + 5×18 = 306px. SO IT WRAPS, to five and one.
   That is the honest trade and it is the right way round: six 44px targets
   with 8px between them need 304px and the row has 281, so one row of six
   was never compatible with the touch floor. If someone later "fixes" the
   wrap by tightening this gap, they are re-breaking the target spacing —
   move the swatches out of the nested card instead. */
.swatches { gap: 18px; }
.sc {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 3px rgba(255,255,255,.28),
              inset 0 -3px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.35), var(--el-1);
}
.sc::before { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: -5px; }
.sc.on::after {
  content: "✓"; font-size: 17px; font-weight: 700; color: #fff; line-height: 1;
  text-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.9), 0 0 4px rgba(0,0,0,.8);
}


/* ══ 10. PICKERS — .pk, .pkr, .pbtn ═══════════════════════════════════════
   THREE REAL BUGS DIE HERE, all found by reading the renderers rather than by
   looking at the screen:

   1. `.pk` never declared a colour, so it inherits the UA button default.
      There is no `color-scheme` declaration anywhere in this app — I grepped
      all four stylesheets — so `buttontext` resolves to BLACK. It is masked
      on the button-picker grid only because every child overrides it and the
      glyph SVGs carry their own fills. It is NOT masked on the Computer
      Vision region sheet: phone-sections.js:8508 emits a full-width `.pk`
      whose `<b>` and `<span>` are direct children with no colour of their
      own. That heading renders black on a dark panel today, at 1.19:1.

   2. `.pk` and `.pbtn` both set font-family:inherit and forgot font-size, so
      they run at 13.333px — the browser's default button size, and one of the
      ten font sizes counted on the CV screen. Nobody chose it.

   3. That same CV sheet marks the chosen kind with `.pk.on`, and NO RULE FOR
      `.pk.on` EXISTS anywhere in the app. The selected region kind has been
      visually identical to the unselected ones. Fixed below, on the same
      three channels as `.pk.cur`.

   .pk and .pkr use the CARD face rather than the control face, and that is
   deliberate rather than arbitrary: both carry small --muted sub-labels, and
   --muted reads 5.24:1 on a card against 4.93:1 on a control. Both pass; the
   card is the more comfortable of the two at 9.5px. */
.pk {
  width: 58px; min-height: 58px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen), var(--zs2-wall);
  color: var(--text);                  /* fixes the inherited UA black */
  font-size: 12px;                     /* fixes the 13.333px UA default */
  padding: var(--sp-1) 2px; gap: 3px;
  position: relative;
}
.pk .pklab { font-size: var(--fs-micro); color: var(--zs-ink-2); }  /* 9.31:1 */
.pk.wide { width: 84px; }
body.a11y-big .pk { width: 66px; }
body.a11y-big .pk.wide { width: 92px; }
.pk.cur, .pk.on {
  border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--accent);
  padding: 3px 1px;                    /* hold the box the same size */
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ion-tint), rgba(111, 155, 255,.05));
}
/* The tick goes on the LABEL's ::before, never on the cell's ::after, because
   .pk::after is zs-micro.css:155 — the lock ring it flashes when a button is
   chosen. Two of the four directions put a tick there; both would have
   inherited that rule's `opacity:0` (so the tick would never have painted)
   AND destroyed the ring's geometry on precisely the cell that gets locked.
   Where a cell is glyph-only and emits no .pklab the tick is simply absent
   and the 2px border carries the state, which is the graceful degradation
   this whole file is written to. */
.pk.cur .pklab::before, .pk.on .pklab::before {
  content: "✓ "; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700;
}
.pkgrid { gap: var(--sp-2); }

.pkr {
  min-height: 60px; padding: var(--sp-2) 14px; gap: 14px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen), var(--zs2-wall);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
}
/* The leading glyph gets the same container the menu rows use, so a picker
   row and a settings row look like relatives. */
.pkr .pki {
  width: 36px; height: 36px; flex: none;
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);
}
.pkr .pki svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; }
.pkr .pkn em { color: var(--muted); margin-top: 2px; }
.pkr.cur { border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--accent); padding: 9px 13px; }

/* THE PICKER TRIGGER — shows the current choice, opens the sheet. It was
   40px, so it also missed the floor. */
.pbtn {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-2); gap: var(--sp-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), var(--zs2-wall);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: var(--fs-sub);            /* fixes the 13.333px UA default */
}
.pbtn .plab { font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-row); }
.pbtn .pchev { color: var(--zs-ink-2); font-size: 13px; }
body.a11y-big .pbtn { min-height: 50px; }


/* ══ 11. CHIPS — .tag, .badge, .pill ══════════════════════════════════════
   Six chip types existed at 11 / 11 / 12 / 12 / 12.5 / 11.5px and radii
   6 / 6 / 999 / 999 / 999. Two shapes now, and the shape is the meaning:

     rounded rect (--r-chip)   METADATA — a fact about the thing
     pill (--r-pill)           STATUS   — something true right now

   OUTLINE CHIPS ARE SAFE HERE, and I verified it rather than assuming it: all
   six .tag call sites across both renderers emit a <span>, none has a click
   handler, and no querySelectorAll('.tag') exists. A chip is static metadata,
   so 1.4.11 does not apply and a 2.30:1 boundary is legal. IF ONE IS EVER
   MADE TAPPABLE it needs --zs-edge, not --line.

   --muted on the card behind an outline chip is 5.24:1. A filled chip would
   have put it on a lifted face; the fill goes, the border stays.

   .pill and .badge are emitted ZERO times today — .pill only from a gated
   section, and no section carries a gate. Kept correct, not deleted. */
.tag, .badge {
  min-height: 22px; padding: 2px var(--sp-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background: none;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-s);
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center;
}
.pill {
  min-height: 22px; padding: 2px 11px;
  border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-ion-line);
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  color: var(--accent);                    /* 6.70:1 on its own wash */
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
}
/* .tag.accent used the PlayStation blue as a generic "interesting" colour.
   Ion label 6.70:1 on the wash, edge 3.72:1 against the card. */
.tag.accent {
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border-color: var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);
}
/* Green here is semantic (Xbox / live) and never alone — the label says the
   word. #56d364 6.44:1 on its wash; the --green border 6.30:1 against a card.
   NOTE: renderHome's "✓ Playing now" tag carries all three of these as INLINE
   styles, so that one instance keeps its own values regardless. Verified at
   phone.html:1339 — no conflict, it degrades to the same appearance. */
.tag.on {
  background: rgba(63,185,80,.15);
  border-color: var(--green);
  color: var(--live-ink);
}
.badges { gap: 6px; margin-top: 4px; }


/* ══ 12. NOTES, ACCORDIONS, OVERLAYS ══════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── 12.1 THE NOTE — the app's most-emitted component ────────────────────
   207 call sites across the two renderers (170 in phone-sections.js, 37 in
   smart-actions.js), up to nineteen on one screen. Every one was a 12.5px
   grey paragraph inside a rgba(88,166,255,.08) box — the PlayStation family
   colour used as the generic information wash, on Xbox screens too. Ninety
   per cent of the Smart Actions screen is grey text, and this component is
   why.

   A note stops being a box. It becomes a margin note: a 2px rule and an
   indent, which is what a note has looked like in print for four hundred
   years. THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE POINT — when 207 of them stop drawing boxes,
   the things on the screen that ARE boxes finally read as boxes.
     --muted on the page ground 6.45:1 (up from 5.4 on the old blue wash)
     --text on it              17.12:1
     the rule                   2.83:1 */
.note {
  background: none;
  border: none;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: 0;
  padding: 2px 0 2px 14px;
  margin-top: var(--sp-3);
  color: var(--muted);
}
.note b { color: var(--text); font-weight: var(--fw-row); }

/* A WARNING KEEPS ITS BOX, and now it is the ONLY note with one — which is
   the entire reason warnings were invisible before: every note looked like a
   callout, so a callout meant nothing. warnNotes() already prefixes "⚠", so
   the signal is glyph + box + rule + colour, never colour alone.
     --muted on the amber wash 5.67:1 · --text 15.04:1
     the 3px amber rule        8.88:1 vs the page · 7.80:1 vs its own wash
   The thin box border is decorative at 2.57:1 and says so; the rule and the
   glyph are what carry the meaning. */
.note.warn {
  background: rgba(224,163,58,.10);
  border: 1px solid rgba(224,163,58,.45);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--amber);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  padding: 12px 14px;
}
.note.warn b { color: var(--amber); }
.lead { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 var(--sp-2); }

/* ── 12.2 THE ACCORDION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The header is a key, the body is the recess it was covering — which is
   literally what an accordion does, and it makes "there is more under here"
   legible before the tap. The renderer puts the title as a DIRECT text child
   of .ah (verified: acc() at phone-sections.js:253), so the header type is
   set on .ah itself in §2 and the chevron and icon are re-sized here. */
.acc > .ah {
  min-height: 58px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3); gap: var(--sp-2);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), inset 0 -1px 0 var(--zs2-cut);
}
.acc > .ah .aicon {
  width: 34px; height: 34px; flex: none;
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);
}
.acc > .ah .aicon svg { width: 18px; height: 18px; }
.acc.open > .ah .aicon { color: var(--accent); }   /* and the chevron rotation
                                                      carries the same state
                                                      without any colour */
.acc > .ah .chev { color: var(--zs-ink-2); font-size: 17px; }
.acc > .ah:active, .acc > .ah.zs-pressing { box-shadow: var(--zs2-sunk); }
/* Open: the body sinks into a well, so "expanded" is legible from the
   silhouette rather than only from the height. */
.acc.open > .ab {
  padding: 14px var(--sp-3) var(--sp-3);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  box-shadow: inset 0 3px 6px -2px rgba(0,0,0,.55);
}
.acc.open > .ab > :first-child { margin-top: 0; }
.acc, .acc + .acc { margin-top: var(--sp-2); }
body.a11y-big .acc > .ah { min-height: 64px; }

/* ── 12.3 THE VIZBOX — a screen, not a card ─────────────────────────────
   .vizbox holds live pad art, response curves and the CV picture. A screen is
   recessed and dark; that alone stops it being confused with the .grp-card
   sitting under it, and it is emitted 17 times so the distinction earns its
   keep. */
.vizbox {
  background: var(--zs2-well-pg);
  background-color: var(--panel);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  padding: var(--sp-3);
  margin-top: var(--sp-3);
}

/* ── 12.4 THE SHEET ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   zs-brand.css owns its glass and its drag grabber (.sheet .shead::before is
   NOT touched here). This is geometry, type and one control that missed the
   floor: .sclose rendered at 38px. */
.sheet { border-radius: var(--r-sheet) var(--r-sheet) 0 0; }
.sheet .shead { padding: 20px var(--sp-3) var(--sp-2); }
.sheet .shead h3 { color: var(--text); flex: 1; }
.sheet .shead .sclose {
  width: 44px; height: 44px; margin: -2px -2px -2px 0;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2); font-size: 16px;
}
.sheet .sbody { padding: 2px var(--sp-3) 22px; }

/* ── 12.5 THE TOAST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   A small floating chip, so it takes the pill shape rather than a card
   radius — it is not a panel and should not look like one. Its button had
   `padding:8px` and no min-height, computing to about 40px; that is the last
   sub-44px target in the kit.
     --text on the toast fill #1d2530 13.42:1 · the ion action 8.78:1 */
.toast {
  border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  min-height: 48px; padding: var(--sp-2) 20px;
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
  gap: var(--sp-3);
}
.toast button {
  min-height: 44px; min-width: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-2);
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: 700;
}


/* ══ 13. VERTICAL RHYTHM ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
   phone.html patches spacing with ten adjacency rules because there is no
   owned stack. Those rules are (0,2,0) and load before us, so they beat a
   plain `.grp-card{margin-top}`. Rather than fight them with !important, the
   same adjacencies are restated here at the same specificity in the right
   order: everything gets the section gap, and then anything immediately after
   a header gets nothing, because the header already spent that space in §4.

   ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING IN THIS BLOCK. The reset must come second.

   Deliberately NOT `.grp + *, .sh + * { margin-top: 0 !important }` — a
   universal sibling with !important zeroes margins on elements it was never
   shown, and one of the four directions shipped exactly that. */
.note + .grp-card, .vizbox + .grp-card, .acc + .grp-card,
.btn + .grp-card, label + .grp-card,
.grp-card + .note, .grp-card + .btn, .grp-card + .acc,
.note + .btn, .btn + .btn, .note + .acc, .acc + .note {
  margin-top: var(--sp-3);
}
.sh + .grp-card, .sh + .acc, .sh + .vizbox, .sh + .note, .sh + .btn,
.sh + .cards, .sh + .seg2, .sh + .lead, .sh + .card, .sh + .addbtn,
.sh + .aimpager, .sh + .prog, .sh + .ph,
.grp + .drow, .grp + .cfg, .grp + .hero, .grp + .item, .grp + .grp-card,
.grp + .acc, .grp + .note, .pkh + .pkgrid {
  margin-top: 0;
}
.dbody { padding-left: 18px; padding-right: 18px; }


/* ══ 14. ACCESSIBILITY ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Six signals, each honoured on its own terms. None is treated as a
   downgrade — high contrast gets a BETTER edge, not a cheaper one. */

/* ── 14.1 MORE CONTRAST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Both edge tiers are promoted and the decorative gradients come off, because
   a gradient is exactly the thing that blurs a boundary for someone who asked
   for boundaries. RE-MEASURED against the 2026-08-18 ladder — this mode needed
   NO retune, which is the check worth having done rather than assumed:
     --line    #8296b0  6.62:1 vs --bg · 5.87:1 vs a card · 4.34:1 vs a
                        control face. ONE value, phone.html's: this file
                        loads last, and it used to set a dimmer #74829a
                        that silently won over the one phone.html documents
                        (m68, 2026-10-10; test-contrast-mode.js holds them
                        together)
     --zs-edge #8c9bb2  6.98:1 vs --bg · 5.33:1 vs a card · 4.25:1 vs a
                        control top
     --muted becomes #bcc7d3: 8.77:1 on a card, 11.49:1 on the ground,
     6.98:1 on the brighter control face, 5.44:1 on a selected pill — the one
     surface the default --muted is forbidden on, and here it passes.
     IT IS SET IN BOTH BRANCHES NOW. phone.html's :root sets it for
     body.a11y-contrast; the OS-level media query below sets it too, so a user
     who asks their PHONE for more contrast and never opens our settings gets
     the same ink. That asymmetry was real and silent.

   AND THE RAISED / RECESSED LANGUAGE IS RE-EXPRESSED AS BORDER WEIGHT rather
   than merely brightened, so this design's core semantic channel survives for
   the users who most need it:
     keys  2px --text  16.57:1 against a key face
     slabs 1px --muted 10.68:1 against --panel
     wells 1px --muted, inset
   That is the single best idea any of the four directions had, and it costs
   this file nothing to take. */
@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
  :root {
    --line: #8296b0; --zs-edge: #8c9bb2; --zs-edge-hi: #a4b2c6;
    --zs-ink-2: #d7e1ec; --muted: #bcc7d3;
  }
}
body.a11y-contrast {
  --line: #8296b0; --zs-edge: #8c9bb2; --zs-edge-hi: #a4b2c6;
  --zs-ink-2: #d7e1ec; --muted: #bcc7d3;
}

@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
  .hero, .cfg, .drow, .item, .grp-card, .acc, .vizbox, .tile, .card,
  .mechcurve, .note, .ph {
    background-image: none; box-shadow: none;
    border-color: var(--muted);
  }
  .btn, .ib, .pbtn, .pk, .pkr, .stp button, .acc > .ah, .hero .go,
  .newcfg, .addbtn, .mechpill, .pback, .appbar .cogbtn,
  .sheet .shead .sclose, .grp button.gact {
    background-image: none; background-color: var(--panel2);
    border: 2px solid var(--text); box-shadow: none; color: var(--text);
  }
  .seg2, .stp, .sw, .prog, .tag, .badge, .hero .live,
  select.sel, input.txt, .vizbox {
    background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--muted); box-shadow: none;
  }
  body .btn.primary { border-color: var(--text); text-shadow: none; }
  body .seg2 button.on, body .subtabs button.on, body #edtabs button.on {
    background-image: none; background-color: var(--panel2);
    border: 2px solid var(--text);
    box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--accent);
  }
  .hero::before, .hero::after { display: none; }   /* no wash under text */
  .tile.on, .pk.cur, .pk.on, .pkr.cur { border-width: 3px; }
  .note { border-left-width: 3px; }
  .fld, .lrow, .stat { border-color: var(--muted); box-shadow: none; }
  /* DESTRUCTIVE KEEPS ITS FILL IN HIGH CONTRAST. The generic key rule above
     flattens every .btn to the control face — which used to be right, because
     danger was a wash and a wash is decoration. It is a SOLID ROLE now, and
     the one control class whose whole job is being identifiable across a room
     must not be the one that goes grey when a user asks for MORE contrast.
     #ffffff on #cf3f45 is 4.72:1 and the 2px --text border this mode gives
     every key rides on top, so the fill is bounded as well as coloured. */
  body .btn.danger, body .ib.danger {
    background-image: none;
    background-color: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45);
    color: var(--btn-3-ink, #ffffff);
    border: 2px solid var(--text);
  }
  body .btn.caution {
    background-image: none; background-color: var(--bg);
    color: var(--red-ink); border: 2px solid var(--red);
  }
}
/* The app's own switch, restated so it always wins over the OS's — the
   behaviour a user who set both would expect. */
body.a11y-contrast .hero, body.a11y-contrast .cfg, body.a11y-contrast .drow,
body.a11y-contrast .item, body.a11y-contrast .grp-card, body.a11y-contrast .acc,
body.a11y-contrast .vizbox, body.a11y-contrast .tile, body.a11y-contrast .card,
body.a11y-contrast .mechcurve, body.a11y-contrast .note, body.a11y-contrast .ph {
  background-image: none; box-shadow: none; border-color: var(--muted);
}
body.a11y-contrast .btn, body.a11y-contrast .ib, body.a11y-contrast .pbtn,
body.a11y-contrast .pk, body.a11y-contrast .pkr, body.a11y-contrast .stp button,
body.a11y-contrast .acc > .ah, body.a11y-contrast .hero .go,
body.a11y-contrast .newcfg, body.a11y-contrast .addbtn,
body.a11y-contrast .mechpill, body.a11y-contrast .pback,
body.a11y-contrast .appbar .cogbtn, body.a11y-contrast .sheet .shead .sclose {
  background-image: none; background-color: var(--panel2);
  border: 2px solid var(--text); box-shadow: none; color: var(--text);
}
body.a11y-contrast .seg2, body.a11y-contrast .stp, body.a11y-contrast .sw,
body.a11y-contrast .prog, body.a11y-contrast .tag, body.a11y-contrast .badge,
body.a11y-contrast .hero .live, body.a11y-contrast select.sel,
body.a11y-contrast input.txt, body.a11y-contrast .vizbox {
  background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--muted); box-shadow: none;
}
body.a11y-contrast .btn.primary { border-color: var(--text); text-shadow: none; }
body.a11y-contrast .seg2 button.on, body.a11y-contrast .subtabs button.on,
body.a11y-contrast #edtabs button.on {
  background-image: none; background-color: var(--panel2);
  border: 2px solid var(--text);
  box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--accent);
}
body.a11y-contrast .hero::before, body.a11y-contrast .hero::after { display: none; }
body.a11y-contrast .fld, body.a11y-contrast .lrow, body.a11y-contrast .stat {
  border-color: var(--muted); box-shadow: none;
}
/* the app's own switch, same reasoning as the @media twin above */
body.a11y-contrast .btn.danger, body.a11y-contrast .ib.danger {
  background-image: none;
  background-color: var(--btn-3-bg, #cf3f45);
  color: var(--btn-3-ink, #ffffff);
  border: 2px solid var(--text);
}
body.a11y-contrast .btn.caution {
  background-image: none; background-color: var(--bg);
  color: var(--red-ink); border: 2px solid var(--red);
}
/* In this mode --muted is #bcc7d3, which is BRIGHTER than --zs-ink-2's normal
   value — so hand the secondary slots back to it rather than capping them. */
body.a11y-contrast .ib, body.a11y-contrast .pk .pklab,
body.a11y-contrast .pbtn .pchev, body.a11y-contrast .btn.ghost,
body.a11y-contrast .stp .sv::after, body.a11y-contrast .stat,
body.a11y-contrast .mechcurve span { color: var(--muted); }

/* ── 14.2 REDUCED TRANSPARENCY ──────────────────────────────────────────
   Every overlay in this file composites onto an OPAQUE parent, so almost
   nothing here is see-through in the sense the setting means. What goes is
   the handful of places a translucent surface stacks on another one — the
   well drawn over the sticky sub-tab strip, the tinted press states, the
   grooves — and the ion wash on the hero. */
@media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
  .subtabs::before { background: var(--panel); }
  .seg2, .stp, .sw, input[type=range].rng, .prog,
  select.sel, input.txt, .vizbox, .ph, .hero .live { box-shadow: none; }
  .hero::before { display: none; }
  .fld.tapper:active, .acc > .ah:active { background-color: var(--panel2); }
  .newcfg, .addbtn { background: var(--panel); box-shadow: none; }
  /* Reduced transparency takes the HATCH off (it is an overlay), never the
     fill — the fill was never translucent and the role depends on it. */
  .btn.danger, .ib.danger { background-image: none; }
  .btn.caution { background-image: none; background-color: var(--caution-bg); }
  .acc.open > .ab { background: var(--bg); box-shadow: none; }
  .lrow, .stat, .fld { box-shadow: none; }
}

/* ── 14.3 REDUCED MOTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────
   This file adds no animation and no transition of its own — zs-brand.css §7
   and zs-micro.css already cover the press springs and the one-shots, and
   body.a11y-motion kills every transition and animation in the document with
   !important. The only motion-adjacent things added here are static ticks on
   a selected tile or picker cell, and a tick that simply exists cannot
   trigger anything. The rules below are on the record rather than implied,
   and they make sure the press FEEDBACK still lands: reduced motion means a
   gentler equivalent, never nothing, so a key that cannot travel still
   answers the finger by swapping its wall for a groove. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .tile.on::after, .sc.on::after, .hero::after, .hero::before {
    transition: none; animation: none;
  }
  .btn:active, .ib:active, .pbtn:active, .pk:active, .pkr:active,
  .stp button:active, .hero .go:active, .acc > .ah:active,
  .btn.zs-pressing, .ib.zs-pressing, .pk.zs-pressing, .pkr.zs-pressing {
    box-shadow: var(--zs2-sunk);
  }
}
body.a11y-motion .btn:active, body.a11y-motion .ib:active,
body.a11y-motion .pk:active, body.a11y-motion .pkr:active,
body.a11y-motion .stp button:active, body.a11y-motion .hero .go:active,
body.a11y-motion .zs-pressing {
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sunk);
}

/* ── 14.4 BIGGER TOUCH TARGETS (the app's own switch) ───────────────────
   phone.html grows .ib, .sw, .stp button and .pk under body.a11y-big at
   (0,2,0), which already outranks this file's (0,1,0) sizes — nothing to
   redo there, and the per-component a11y-big rules above sit beside their
   own components. These are the ones phone.html missed. */
body.a11y-big .btn { min-height: 58px; }
body.a11y-big .btn.sm { min-height: 48px; }
body.a11y-big .fld { min-height: 68px; }
body.a11y-big .lrow { min-height: 60px; }
body.a11y-big .hero .go { min-height: 64px; }
body.a11y-big .pback, body.a11y-big .grp button.gact { min-height: 50px; }

/* AND THE GAPS GROW TOO — this mode raised every cap and not one space
   between them, so the six adjacency defects the touch audit found all got
   WORSE in the mode a user turns on precisely because they are missing
   targets. Every value here is derived, not chosen: --sp-1h goes to 12px, so
   the segment strips, the .rt key clusters and the swatch row all inherit
   the wider clear air from the tokens they already use. The two places that
   hardcode their own separation get the matching bump by hand. */
body.a11y-big { --sp-1h: 12px; }
body.a11y-big .lrow .rt .ib + .ib { margin-left: 14px; }
body.a11y-big .swatches { gap: 22px; }

/* ── 14.5 OLED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   --panel drops to #0a0d11 and --bg to true black. Because every surface
   above is an OVERLAY rather than a hex fill, the whole system moves with it
   for free — the same RELATIVE lift, not a frozen copy of the default theme.

   THE ALPHAS ARE RESTATED HERE EVEN WHERE THEY NOW MATCH §1.5, and that is
   deliberate. Before 2026-08-18 this block existed to push OLED HIGHER than
   the base, because black is less forgiving than near-black. The new base
   ladder has caught up with (and passed) those numbers, so an override left
   at its old value would now DARKEN OLED instead of lifting it — the exact
   inversion that kills a theme quietly. Pinning the measured values keeps
   this mode solved on its own terms whichever way the base moves next.

   Composited on the OLED --panel #0a0d11:
     card top #1c1f23 · card bottom #111418
     ctl  top #2e3034 · ctl  bottom #1a1d20
     selected pill     #3f4144
     well on the page  #090909  (on true black, where it is used)
   Card-vs-black 1.24:1 and card-vs-control 1.25:1 — the same relationships
   the default theme now has, which is the whole point of overlays.

   The wells still go all the way down: an OLED recess should be a hole. */
body.zs-oled {
  --zs2-card-a: rgba(255,255,255,.075);
  --zs2-card-b: rgba(255,255,255,.030);
  --zs2-ctl-a:  rgba(255,255,255,.145);
  --zs2-ctl-b:  rgba(255,255,255,.065);
  --zs2-sel:    rgba(255,255,255,.215);
  --zs2-well:   rgba(0,0,0,.62);
}

/* ── 14.6 FOCUS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The ring itself is zs-brand.css's — 2px ion at 11.20:1 against the ground
   with a 5px wash halo, and it is not touched.

   WHAT IS FIXED is phone.html:518, which sets `border-radius:4px` on EVERY
   :focus-visible. A focus ring that reshapes the element it is pointing at
   makes the target harder to find, which is precisely backwards for the
   keyboard and switch-access users this ring exists for. Each family gets its
   own shape back. */
.btn:focus-visible, .btn.sm:focus-visible, .newcfg:focus-visible,
.addbtn:focus-visible, .seg2 button:focus-visible,
.subtabs button:focus-visible, #edtabs button:focus-visible,
.pbtn:focus-visible, .pk:focus-visible, .stp button:focus-visible,
select.sel:focus-visible, input.txt:focus-visible,
.pback:focus-visible, .safil button:focus-visible {
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
}
.grp-card:focus-visible, .card:focus-visible, .tile:focus-visible,
.cfg:focus-visible, .drow:focus-visible, .item:focus-visible,
.pkr:focus-visible, .acc > .ah:focus-visible, .vizbox:focus-visible,
.lrow:focus-visible {
  border-radius: var(--r-card); outline-offset: -3px;
}
.ib:focus-visible, .sw:focus-visible, .sc:focus-visible, .tag:focus-visible,
.mechpill:focus-visible, .grp .gact:focus-visible,
.appbar .cogbtn:focus-visible, .sheet .shead .sclose:focus-visible,
.toast button:focus-visible {
  border-radius: var(--r-pill);
}
.hero:focus-visible, .hero .go:focus-visible {
  border-radius: 0 0 var(--r-card) var(--r-card); outline-offset: -3px;
}


/* ══ 15. THE TWO RUNTIME-INJECTED STYLESHEETS, RECONCILED ═════════════════
   smart-actions.js appends <style id="zs-smart-css"> and phone-sections.js
   appends <style id="cv-region-css"> to document.head AT RUNTIME, so both
   land after every file in <head>. Loading from the BODY is what lets these
   rules win on plain source order instead of needing prefixes — and it is why
   only the genuine defects are corrected here.

   The rest of those blocks is left alone on purpose: rewriting a stylesheet
   this file cannot see the whole of is how a design system breaks a screen it
   was never shown. `cv-region-css` in particular is a drag-handle overlay
   whose blue / dashed / dotted / green box states are a DIAGRAM, not chrome —
   the hues there encode region kind and hit state, so they are signal and
   stay untouched. */

/* .satile.sel used rgba(88,166,255,.35) — the PlayStation family colour — as
   a generic "this one is selected" glow, on every screen including Xbox
   configs. Ion, plus a width change so the state is not colour alone. */
.satile.sel {
  border-color: var(--accent);
  border-width: 2px;
  box-shadow: inset 0 2px 0 var(--accent), 0 0 0 2px var(--zs-ion-glow);
}
/* .satile.now means "running right now" — green is correct and the row says
   so in words. It only needs the shape channel. */
.satile.now { border-width: 2px; box-shadow: inset 0 2px 0 var(--green); }
.satile {
  border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  border-color: var(--zs-edge);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);
}
/* .sapreview is a card wearing the PlayStation wash for the same reason. */
.sapreview {
  background: none;
  border: none;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--zs2-ion-line);
  border-radius: 0;
  padding-left: 14px;
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);
}
/* The family/filter chips and the pills inherit the kit's two shapes and its
   44px floor. .safil button was 8px/13px padding with a 12.5px label, which
   computes to about 35px. */
.safil button, .sapill {
  border-radius: var(--r-pill); min-height: 44px;
  border-color: var(--zs-edge);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  color: var(--zs-ink-2);
}
body .safil button.on {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-sel), var(--zs2-ctl-a));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  border-color: var(--zs-edge);
  color: var(--text); font-weight: 700;
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-lit), inset 0 -3px 0 var(--accent);
}
.sacat, .sastripbox { border-radius: var(--r-card); border-color: var(--line); }
.sacat b { font-size: 14px; font-weight: var(--fw-row); color: var(--text); }
.sacat i, .safam i { color: var(--zs-ink-2); }
.sacat .saci, .safam .safi { color: var(--accent); }
.lrow.seqline { border-left-color: var(--zs-edge); }
.hmrow { min-height: 60px; }
.grp-card.saoff { opacity: .55; }


/* ══ 16. THE AIM PAGER ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The best-designed screen in the app and the only place a card layout
   already existed — so this is type, targets and materials, never layout. Its
   own (0,2,0)/(0,3,0) overrides in phone.html keep the centred-stack, and
   these match that specificity so the sizes move with the rest of the scale
   while the layout does not.

   The hip/ADS header colours stay exactly as they are: white on #8a6410 is
   5.37:1 and on #1a6e30 is 6.33:1, and they are that screen's own semantic. */
.aimph {
  border-radius: var(--r-card); padding: 13px var(--sp-3);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.22),
              inset 0 -2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.30), var(--el-1);
}
.aimph em { font-size: var(--fs-sub); opacity: .95; }
.aimpage .fld .flab { font-size: 15px; font-weight: var(--fw-row); }
.aimpage .fld .flab em { font-size: var(--fs-sub); line-height: var(--lh-sub); }
.aimpage .fld .stp .sv { font-size: 19px; font-weight: 700; }
/* phone.html:407 shrinks the stepper keys to 44x40 inside an aim page — under
   the floor on the axis a thumb actually misses, on the Aim screen, which is
   the core accommodation in the whole product. Restated at matching
   specificity, because at (0,1,0) it would silently lose. */
.aimpage .fld .stp button { width: 46px; height: 46px; font-size: 21px; }
/* …and the rule above then OUT-RANKS body.a11y-big's own `.stp button{50px}`,
   which is (0,2,0). Caught by measuring in the browser, not by reading: with
   Bigger touch targets ON, the Aim stepper was coming back at 46px instead of
   growing. A fix that quietly shrinks a target for the user who asked for
   bigger ones is worse than the bug it replaced, so it is restored here at
   (0,4,0) — and above 50px, because this is the screen where it matters. */
body.a11y-big .aimpage .fld .stp button { width: 52px; height: 52px; }
/* Large/XL text (body.a11y-text, set by phone.html's applyAccess): the zoomed
   row can no longer fit a label beside the fixed-width stepper — the label
   was collapsing to ONE WORD PER LINE beside it, in exactly the accessibility
   mode this product leads with. Let the row wrap: the label takes the full
   line and the control drops below it with the whole row to spend. Layout
   only — no renderer, label or key changes. */
body.a11y-text .aimpage .fld { flex-wrap: wrap; }
body.a11y-text .aimpage .fld .flab { flex: 1 1 100%; }
/* The stepper rows never give the label even 40% of the row at ANY size —
   46px keys + a 74px value well is a 176px control in a ~313px row, leaving
   a 77px (25%) label column that breaks "Mouse DPI" across two lines at
   Default. Measured in the browser, not guessed. So stepper rows wrap
   always; rows with small controls (switches) keep the side-by-side layout
   until Large/XL (the body.a11y-text rules above). Old engines without
   :has() simply keep the side-by-side row — nothing breaks. */
.aimpage .fld:has(.stp) { flex-wrap: wrap; }
.aimpage .fld:has(.stp) .flab { flex: 1 1 100%; }
.aimdots i { background: var(--line); }        /* 2.83:1 — was 1.38:1 */
.aimdots i.on { background: var(--accent); box-shadow: 0 0 8px var(--zs-ion-glow); }
.smblk { padding: 14px var(--sp-3) 2px; }
.smcar .smval {
  min-width: 150px; min-height: 48px;
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs-edge); border-radius: var(--zs-shear-ctl);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700;
}
.smcar .arr { color: var(--accent); font-size: 26px; }
.smcar .arr:not([disabled]):active { filter: none; opacity: .6; }


/* ══ 17. SECTION TINTS — the colour-separation system (2026-08-18) ═════════
   Owner: "the app needs some more colour … more colour separation" — the
   editor MENU rows carry per-section tinted chips (phone.html ico-*), but
   the screens BEHIND them were monochrome, so "which screen am I on" had no
   colour answer. This section extends each section's established menu hue
   into its own content.

   ── THE CONTRACT (read this before using it from a renderer) ─────────────
   One attribute:   data-tint="overview" | "aim" | "bindings" | "smart"
                              | "vision" | "global"
   Set AUTOMATICALLY by phone.html's openDetail() on #dbody for the five
   editor sections AND for Global Settings — a section renderer gets its
   tint with NO markup change.
   A renderer may ALSO put the attribute on any container of its own (a
   card, a sheet body) to tint just that subtree, or on a neutral screen's
   sub-region if that region is genuinely about one section.

   Inside a tinted subtree these custom properties are live for ad-hoc use
   (e.g. style="color:var(--tint)"):
     --tint          the section's flat hue (glyphs, rails, readouts)
     --tint-wash     its ~13% wash          (chip/selection backgrounds)
     --tint-strong   its ~30% wash          (the ON switch track)
     --tint-edge     its ~50% border        (chip/selection edges)

   The hues are the SAME five the menu chips already ship (phone.html
   ico-*): Overview = the product's Ion, Aim = gold, Keybinds = steel,
   Smart Actions = rose (Auto Actions' own, D127; it was bolt-orange),
   Computer Vision = the premium gradient
   (flat fallback: Ion — the gradient itself appears where a border-image
   can carry it, e.g. the accordion chip below). They dodge the semantic
   set on purpose: blue/green/purple keep meaning PS/Xbox/M&K and are NOT
   legal tint values, ever.

   WHAT A TINT MAY TOUCH (and what this section wires): the corner bracket
   of .sh/.pkh/.grp headers, accordion header icons, and the aim page's
   header rule — i.e. the things that say WHERE YOU ARE.

   WHAT A TINT MUST NEVER TOUCH: anything that says WHAT IS ON or WHAT YOU
   PRESSED. The ON state of .sw/.seg2/.subtabs/#edtabs, the .stp +/- keys,
   .flab .rv readouts and the slider thumb were all tinted here until
   2026-08-28 and are now the accent, once, in zs-fixes §16: a control that
   changes colour by room makes the user re-learn "selected" five times.
   Also never: .btn.primary and the hero's Edit rail (a solid accent cap
   means "press this", app-wide), focus rings (one colour everywhere or
   keyboard users lose the thread), the danger/warn reds and ambers, family
   colours, and any text that carries meaning. Colour is never the only
   signal here — every tinted element sits beside a label that already says
   which section this is. */

[data-tint] {
  --tint: var(--accent);
  --tint-wash: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  --tint-strong: rgba(111, 155, 255,.28);
  --tint-edge: var(--zs2-ion-line);
}
/* Overview IS the product — it keeps Ion, i.e. the defaults above. */
[data-tint="aim"] {
  --tint: var(--gold);
  --tint-wash: rgba(227,179,65,.13);
  --tint-strong: rgba(227,179,65,.30);
  --tint-edge: rgba(227,179,65,.50);
}
/* KEYBINDS IS THE MOUSE-AND-KEYBOARD ROOM, SO IT WEARS M&K VIOLET.
   It used to be --steel #b9c6d8, and steel is not a colour: measured across
   the whole app the Keybinds screen came back 3.0% chromatic pixels — second
   lowest in the product — because data-tint="bindings" WAS being set and
   bought nothing. One of five rooms had no hue at all, which is the same as
   the code having a hole in it.

   THIS IS NOT THE FAMILY SET BEING REPURPOSED, and the distinction is the
   whole justification: --purple means keyboard & mouse, and Keybinds is the
   screen where a key becomes a button. The colour is being used FOR ITS OWN
   MEANING, not borrowed for decoration — which is exactly what the rule
   above ("blue/green/purple are NOT legal tint values") was written to stop.
   Blue and green stay illegal as tints: no room is "the PlayStation room".
     #a371f7 measures 5.30:1 on a card; --purple-ink #b98cff 5.17:1 on a
     control face, which is where the wash-backed chips sit. */
[data-tint="bindings"] {
  --tint: var(--purple);
  --tint-wash: rgba(163,113,247,.14);
  --tint-strong: rgba(163,113,247,.30);
  --tint-edge: rgba(163,113,247,.52);
}
/* SMART ACTIONS WEARS AUTO ACTIONS' ROSE (D127). The owner: "make the Smart
   Actions tab the same type of red as the Auto Actions, I like that theme."
   Two rooms, one hue, and that is not the "five rooms, four colours" defect
   above: Auto Actions IS the Smart Actions room, scoped to one weapon (D122
   section 3), so the same editor, list and library now look the same in
   both doors. The values are auto-actions.js's `html [data-tint="auto"]`
   exactly; --rose lives on phone.html's :root. It was bolt orange #ff9457.
     rose 8.03:1 on the page, 7.12:1 on a card; --accent-ink on it 7.66:1. */
[data-tint="smart"] {
  --tint: var(--rose, #f47fa4);
  --tint-wash: rgba(244,127,164,.13);
  --tint-strong: rgba(244,127,164,.30);
  --tint-edge: rgba(244,127,164,.50);
}
/* SCREEN ASSIST IS CYAN, AND THAT IS WHAT FINALLY SEPARATES IT FROM OVERVIEW.
   It took the middle stop of the premium gradient, #6f9bff — which is also
   --accent, which is also Overview's tint. Five rooms, four colours: "the
   blue one" identified nothing, and the accent, the colour that is supposed
   to mean "press this", was doubling as a room's name.
   Cyan is legal and it is free. Legal: the reserved set is blue/green/purple
   (PS / Xbox / M&K) plus the warning amber and the error red, and cyan is
   none of them. Free: it used to be the top and foot of every primary button
   in the app (#3ee3d8 → #26c4ba), and that cap is now a ramp of the accent
   instead (phone.html :root --cap-*), so no action wears cyan any more.
     #5ccfd6 measures 9.60:1 on a card, 10.83:1 on the page. */
[data-tint="vision"] {
  --tint: var(--tint-vision);
  --tint-wash: rgba(92,207,214,.13);
  --tint-strong: rgba(92,207,214,.28);
  --tint-edge: rgba(92,207,214,.50);
}
/* GLOBAL SETTINGS IS NOT A ROOM, SO IT WEARS THE NEUTRAL.
   Measured, its Performance tab was the least colourful screen in the whole
   app (meanChroma 11.5) and its active tab was ION — the same treatment as
   Overview's, so device settings and config settings were indistinguishable
   at a glance. Steel is the hue Keybinds just vacated and it is the honest
   one here: this screen is about the BOX, not about a config, and hardware
   is neutral. It is a real step of colour separation without adding a sixth
   hue to a five-hue code.
     --steel #b9c6d8 measures 10.27:1 on a card — it is an ink-grade value,
     which is why it can carry a tick and a rail as well as an edge. */
[data-tint="global"] {
  --tint: var(--steel, #b9c6d8);
  --tint-wash: rgba(185,198,216,.13);
  --tint-strong: rgba(185,198,216,.30);
  --tint-edge: rgba(185,198,216,.46);
}

/* the header bracket: the one mark that repeats down a whole screen, so it
   is the cheapest "you are on the gold screen" signal available */
[data-tint] .sh::before, [data-tint] .pkh::before, [data-tint] .grp::before {
  border-color: var(--tint);
}
/* accordion header icons wear the section chip treatment the menu rows
   established — same wash, same edge, same glyph colour */
[data-tint] .acc > .ah .aicon {
  background: var(--tint-wash);
  border-color: var(--tint-edge);
  color: var(--tint);
}
/* Screen Assist's accordion chip used to be the brand gradient in its border
   with the glyph left --text — the one room whose chip did not look like the
   other five, on top of sharing Overview's hue. It takes the same wash / edge
   / glyph treatment as every other room, in cyan. */
/* THE STATE CHANNELS LEFT THIS SECTION (2026-08-28).
   Five rules used to live here — the live readout, the stepper keys, the ON
   switch, the selected segment/tab and the slider thumb — each re-inked in
   the room's hue. Measured on the running app that gave the SAME segmented
   control four different "selected" colours on four screens (teal / gold /
   orange / blue), and the same for every switch and every thumb, so "which
   one is on" had to be re-learnt in every room. They are one rule each now,
   in the accent, in zs-fixes.css §16 — read the argument there. A tint may
   still say WHERE YOU ARE; it may not say WHAT IS ON. Everything left in
   this section is the first kind: the header bracket, the accordion chip and
   the aim page's rule. */
/* the aim page's stance-header rule joins its own screen's tint */
[data-tint] .aimph::after {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--tint-edge), transparent);
}

/* ── 17.1 THE ACCESSIBILITY MODES KEEP THEIR FLATTENING ──────────────────
   §14 strips washes and gradients for the users who asked for boundaries;
   the tint rules above load later and would quietly paint them back. These
   restatements keep the flattened look and keep ONLY the strong indicators
   — the 3px rail and the tinted border — which is the same trade §14
   already makes with the accent. Same selectors, later in the file, so
   they win the tie the way §14's own rules do. */
@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
  body [data-tint] .sw.on { background: var(--bg); }
  body [data-tint] .seg2 button.on, body [data-tint] .subtabs button.on,
  body [data-tint] #edtabs button.on {
    background-image: none; background-color: var(--panel2);
    border: 2px solid var(--text);
    box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--tint);
  }
  body [data-tint] .acc > .ah .aicon { background: var(--bg); border-color: var(--muted); }
  .art.mkart { background-image: none; }
  .art.mkart .gm0 { display: none; }
}
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .sw.on { background: var(--bg); }
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .seg2 button.on,
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .subtabs button.on,
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] #edtabs button.on {
  background-image: none; background-color: var(--panel2);
  border: 2px solid var(--text);
  box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--tint);
}
body.a11y-contrast [data-tint] .acc > .ah .aicon {
  background: var(--bg); border-color: var(--muted);
}
body.a11y-contrast .art.mkart { background-image: none; }
body.a11y-contrast .art.mkart .gm0 { display: none; }
